You know, you've probably heard this term, this phrase,

112
00:04:49.820 --> 00:04:52.200
the only way out is through, have you heard that?

113
00:04:52.200 --> 00:04:53.230
Right.
Yeah.

114
00:04:53.230 --> 00:04:57.105
Unfortunately, more times than I care to remember.

115
00:04:57.105 --> 00:04:58.560
(laughs) Yeah, you've lived it.

116
00:04:58.560 --> 00:04:59.660
Yeah.

117
00:04:59.660 --> 00:05:03.970
I mean that's a very optimistic spin on it, of course.

118
00:05:03.970 --> 00:05:05.650
Well, what are the alternatives?

119
00:05:05.650 --> 00:05:06.496
Right.
I mean,

120
00:05:06.496 --> 00:05:09.210
we could either go for it,

121
00:05:09.210 --> 00:05:11.010
or we could be running our whole life.

122
00:05:11.010 --> 00:05:11.843
And I have to say,

123
00:05:11.843 --> 00:05:12.840
running's pretty exhausting

124
00:05:12.840 --> 00:05:14.650
in my experience.
Yeah.

125
00:05:14.650 --> 00:05:17.979
Right, well, you're quite the man for the moment.

126
00:05:17.979 --> 00:05:20.800
I think everything that you've been studying

127
00:05:20.800 --> 00:05:22.170
for many, many years,

128
00:05:22.170 --> 00:05:24.450
is now coming to the forefront

129
00:05:24.450 --> 00:05:27.730
of what people are thinking about and grappling with,

130
00:05:27.730 --> 00:05:30.120
specifically anxiety, you know?

131
00:05:30.120 --> 00:05:32.140
I think it's the rare person

132
00:05:32.140 --> 00:05:35.510
who isn't experiencing some kind of anxiety, you know,

133
00:05:35.510 --> 00:05:38.560
in this predicament that we find ourselves in.

134
00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:39.480
Yeah.

135
00:05:39.480 --> 00:05:41.100
And that's what we're gonna talk about today.

136
00:05:41.100 --> 00:05:43.310
So, maybe the best way to launch into this,

137
00:05:43.310 --> 00:05:45.550
is first to just define our terms.

138
00:05:45.550 --> 00:05:46.870
When we're talking about anxiety,

139
00:05:46.870 --> 00:05:49.710
what are we talking about specifically?

140
00:05:49.710 --> 00:05:51.610
And how does that differ,

141
00:05:51.610 --> 00:05:54.930
from other kind of kindred emotions,

142
00:05:54.930 --> 00:05:58.960
like fear and worry and the like?

143
00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:00.680
Yeah, I think that's a great place to start.

144
00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:02.150
So, if you look at,

145
00:06:02.150 --> 00:06:04.440
I think the dictionary definition of anxiety,

146
00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:06.017
goes something like, you know,

147
00:06:06.017 --> 00:06:09.457
"Feeling of worry, nervousness or unease,

148
00:06:09.457 --> 00:06:10.707
"about an imminent event,

149
00:06:10.707 --> 00:06:13.350
"or something with an uncertain outcome."

150
00:06:13.350 --> 00:06:14.183
Mm hmm.

151
00:06:14.183 --> 00:06:16.870
It's interesting in that definition,

152
00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.770
worry is an interesting word,

153
00:06:19.770 --> 00:06:23.530
because it can be a noun, like this feeling of unease,

154
00:06:23.530 --> 00:06:27.760
but it can also be a verb, where I am worrying.

155
00:06:27.760 --> 00:06:29.930
So, I think, let's bookmark that and come back to that,

156
00:06:29.930 --> 00:06:33.160
because I think that's a really critical distinction,

157
00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:34.660
that in both of those, can actually,

158
00:06:34.660 --> 00:06:36.110
the noun can lead to the verb,

159
00:06:36.110 --> 00:06:39.370
which can feed back to the noun of worrying.

160
00:06:39.370 --> 00:06:43.350
But looking at it from a scientific standpoint, you know,

161
00:06:43.350 --> 00:06:46.680
I think a lot of people associate anxiety,

162
00:06:46.680 --> 00:06:49.180
with something that's kind of necessary,

163
00:06:49.180 --> 00:06:52.397
needed for survival, you know, especially right now.

164
00:06:52.397 --> 00:06:55.070
And that's something that I dove into a lot in my book,

165
00:06:55.070 --> 00:06:59.640
because I've been really interested with this idea,

166
00:06:59.640 --> 00:07:03.040
you know, there's this whole idea of performance anxiety,

167
00:07:03.040 --> 00:07:05.770
and I actually haven't found any evidence to support it.

168
00:07:05.770 --> 00:07:07.460
So, let's bookmark that as well,

169
00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.440
and talk about some of these origins here.

170
00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:10.390
Mm hmm.

171
00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:15.390
So, think of fear as a survival mechanism, okay?

172
00:07:15.800 --> 00:07:18.420
We've talked before about habits,

173
00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:20.520
and setting up habits for survival, right?

174
00:07:20.520 --> 00:07:21.930
To remember where food is,

175
00:07:21.930 --> 00:07:23.600
and to remember where danger is.

176
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:26.580
So, these learning mechanisms go way back, you know,

177
00:07:26.580 --> 00:07:28.550
evolutionarily conserved all the way to the sea slug.

178
00:07:28.550 --> 00:07:30.490
Like this is the oldest learning mechanism

179
00:07:30.490 --> 00:07:31.530
known in science, you know,

180
00:07:31.530 --> 00:07:33.710
positive and negative reinforcement.

181
00:07:33.710 --> 00:07:36.127
So, if you zoom in on fear

182
00:07:36.127 --> 00:07:39.140
as a negatively reinforced behavior,

183
00:07:39.140 --> 00:07:41.100
fear helps us survive, right?

184
00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.060
So, think of our ancient ancestors out on the savanna,

185
00:07:44.060 --> 00:07:46.730
they are forging for food, right?

186
00:07:46.730 --> 00:07:48.450
But they don't know if it's dangerous.

187
00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.090
So, they're moving from their safe zone, their cave,

188
00:07:51.090 --> 00:07:55.270
out into more of an uncertain space, the savanna.

189
00:07:55.270 --> 00:07:57.476
So, their brain naturally goes on high alert,

190
00:07:57.476 --> 00:08:00.000
to start to learn things,

191
00:08:00.000 --> 00:08:02.217
like, "Oh, there's food, go there again.

192
00:08:02.217 --> 00:08:05.190
"There's danger, don't go there again," okay?

193
00:08:05.190 --> 00:08:08.670
So, fear helps us learn where things are safe

194
00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:09.510
and where things aren't safe,

195
00:08:09.510 --> 00:08:12.790
so we can avoid the unsafe places.

196
00:08:12.790 --> 00:08:17.510
That fear mechanism is this old part of the brain,

197
00:08:17.510 --> 00:08:19.180
and then layered on top of it,

198
00:08:19.180 --> 00:08:22.100
is a thinking and planning part of the brain, the neocortex,

199
00:08:22.100 --> 00:08:24.410
or the prefrontal cortex in particular.

200
00:08:24.410 --> 00:08:25.530
And this is interesting,

201
00:08:25.530 --> 00:08:28.700
because it helps us survive in a different way.

202
00:08:28.700 --> 00:08:32.310
It helps us survive through thinking and planning,

203
00:08:32.310 --> 00:08:35.450
yet it needs information,

204
00:08:35.450 --> 00:08:37.830
and preferably accurate information,

205
00:08:37.830 --> 00:08:38.663
so in this day and age

206
00:08:38.663 --> 00:08:42.470
there's a lot of misinformation which gets in the way,

207
00:08:42.470 --> 00:08:45.480
but it also is helpful for it to have precedent.

208
00:08:45.480 --> 00:08:49.340
So, when it's going into unchartered territory,

209
00:08:49.340 --> 00:08:51.370
it's really hard to think and plan, you know?

210
00:08:51.370 --> 00:08:54.600
Like, "Oh, let's, let's go explore Saturn."

211
00:08:54.600 --> 00:08:57.020
Well, you know, we've never done that before,

212
00:08:57.020 --> 00:09:00.530
so you gotta think of a bunch of things,

213
00:09:00.530 --> 00:09:01.510
and try to approximate,

214
00:09:01.510 --> 00:09:02.480
but it'd be much easier

215
00:09:02.480 --> 00:09:03.940
if somebody else had explored Saturn

216
00:09:03.940 --> 00:09:04.940
and wrote a book about it,

217
00:09:04.940 --> 00:09:07.440
said, "Oh do this, don't do that," okay?

218
00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.210
So, the prefrontal cortex, think of fear, helps us survive.

219
00:09:12.210 --> 00:09:14.200
The prefrontal cortex helps us survive,

220
00:09:14.200 --> 00:09:18.700
but if you pair fear with a lack of certainty, right,

221
00:09:18.700 --> 00:09:20.593
which is what the prefrontal cortex is trying to help us do,

222
00:09:20.593 --> 00:09:22.320
it's trying to help us predict the future,

223
00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:24.250
based on past experiences,

224
00:09:24.250 --> 00:09:25.890
if there is no precedent,

225
00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:27.240
if there's a lack of certainty,

226
00:09:27.240 --> 00:09:28.730
that fear, plus uncertainty,

227
00:09:28.730 --> 00:09:29.823
leads to anxiety.

228
00:09:30.860 --> 00:09:33.470
And people think, "Oh, anxiety it's gonna help me survive."

229
00:09:33.470 --> 00:09:35.560
No, it doesn't. (laughs)
Right.

230
00:09:35.560 --> 00:09:38.230
There is no evidence for it helping us survive.

231
00:09:38.230 --> 00:09:40.730
It makes our thinking and planning brain go offline.

232
00:09:40.730 --> 00:09:43.050
And if you think of the extreme form of anxiety,

233
00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:47.120
panic, right, which is wildly unthinking behavior,

234
00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:49.770
that's that far into the spectrum of anxiety.

235
00:09:49.770 --> 00:09:51.040
Right, it's an interesting,

236
00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:54.380
but subtle and important distinction,

237
00:09:54.380 --> 00:09:56.250
in that it is the uncertainty,

238
00:09:56.250 --> 00:09:58.750
that's driving the irrationality, right?

239
00:09:58.750 --> 00:10:00.620
You can be afraid,

240
00:10:00.620 --> 00:10:04.000
when you have a certain set of predictable parameters

241
00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:04.833
to deal with,

242
00:10:04.833 --> 00:10:07.670
but when you don't know what you're venturing into,

243
00:10:07.670 --> 00:10:09.610
that's what provokes the anxiety.

244
00:10:09.610 --> 00:10:12.290
So, to kind of extend that,

245
00:10:12.290 --> 00:10:13.770
what's fascinating about that,

246
00:10:13.770 --> 00:10:18.670
is it's not the dire consequence that creates the anxiety,

247
00:10:18.670 --> 00:10:20.880
it's the lack of certainty,

248
00:10:20.880 --> 00:10:23.920
around whether that consequence is going to be dire,

249
00:10:23.920 --> 00:10:25.690
or not so dire

250
00:10:25.690 --> 00:10:26.523
Yeah, absolutely.

251
00:10:26.523 --> 00:10:28.930
And just to be super clear for your listeners,

252
00:10:28.930 --> 00:10:31.650
it's not that fear is a problem, right?

253
00:10:31.650 --> 00:10:34.983
Fear helps us learn, in new situations in particular.

254
00:10:36.120 --> 00:10:40.910
But fear doesn't have the same neurochemical reaction

255
00:10:40.910 --> 00:10:42.430
in the brain as anxiety.

256
00:10:42.430 --> 00:10:44.840
Like, neurochemically, like how do these two things

257
00:10:44.840 --> 00:10:46.480
distinguish themselves?

258
00:10:46.480 --> 00:10:48.760
Yeah, I don't think all of the neurochemistry

259
00:10:48.760 --> 00:10:49.593
has been worked out,

260
00:10:49.593 --> 00:10:51.370
but I think one way to think about this,

261
00:10:51.370 --> 00:10:53.060
is on a temporal scale.

262
00:10:53.060 --> 00:10:54.610
So, if you look at the timescales,

263
00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:56.557
you can actually differentiate them pretty well.

264
00:10:56.557 --> 00:10:58.100
Right.
I'll use an example,

265
00:10:58.100 --> 00:10:59.870
of let's say stepping out into the street.

266
00:10:59.870 --> 00:11:01.830
So, let's say in this day and age,

267
00:11:01.830 --> 00:11:04.430
when everybody is distracted by their,

268
00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.017
what does Cornell Welch say,

269
00:11:06.017 --> 00:11:07.780
"Our weapons of mass distraction,"

270
00:11:07.780 --> 00:11:09.830
you know, everybody's looking at their phone.

271
00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.610
So, if somebody steps out in the street,

272
00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:13.420
and doesn't see the bus coming, you know,

273
00:11:13.420 --> 00:11:14.650
bearing down on them,

274
00:11:14.650 --> 00:11:16.130
they instinctively jump back

275
00:11:16.130 --> 00:11:17.470
onto the safety of the sidewalk.

276
00:11:17.470 --> 00:11:18.530
And I say instinctively,

277
00:11:18.530 --> 00:11:21.490
because this is much faster than our thinking brain.

278
00:11:21.490 --> 00:11:23.330
You know, imagine you look up to the bus,

279
00:11:23.330 --> 00:11:26.237
and go, "Hmm, is that really going fast enough, you know?

280
00:11:26.237 --> 00:11:28.200
"Is it going to veer," splat.

281
00:11:28.200 --> 00:11:29.244
Right.
You know?

282
00:11:29.244 --> 00:11:30.537
We don't have time for that.
Right.

283
00:11:30.537 --> 00:11:33.020
So, we jump back onto the safety of the sidewalk,

284
00:11:33.020 --> 00:11:34.907
and then we have a fear response.

285
00:11:34.907 --> 00:11:35.740
(air whooshing)

286
00:11:35.740 --> 00:11:36.920
All of our, you know,

287
00:11:36.920 --> 00:11:39.100
basically our fight or flight response kicks in,

288
00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:42.100
and says, "Wow, that was crazy."

289
00:11:42.100 --> 00:11:43.730
And I, to be more precise,

290
00:11:43.730 --> 00:11:45.630
that fight or flight response,

291
00:11:45.630 --> 00:11:47.460
is where we get these catecholamines,

292
00:11:47.460 --> 00:11:50.650
we get all these, basically the adrenaline surge,

293
00:11:50.650 --> 00:11:53.830
that says, "Hey, you gotta run if you need to,

294
00:11:53.830 --> 00:11:56.424
"if you're not safe at this point, okay?"

295
00:11:56.424 --> 00:11:57.370
Right.

296
00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:00.490
But that also helps us have this fear response,

297
00:12:00.490 --> 00:12:02.977
that says, "Wow, you could have almost gotten killed.

298
00:12:02.977 --> 00:12:05.747
"You should learn from this, look both ways," you know,

299
00:12:05.747 --> 00:12:07.760
"Relearn what you learned as a kid."

300
00:12:07.760 --> 00:12:11.680
The problem is, so think of that as super rapid,

301
00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.070
is that instinctual response,

302
00:12:14.070 --> 00:12:16.860
the rapid response is that fear response,

303
00:12:16.860 --> 00:12:20.750
but then ideally that drains out of our system, you know,

304
00:12:20.750 --> 00:12:23.420
and we move on when we've learned.

305
00:12:23.420 --> 00:12:24.287
Right.

306
00:12:24.287 --> 00:12:26.200
So, this is what differentiates that from anxiety,

307
00:12:26.200 --> 00:12:27.930
is with the anxiety,

308
00:12:27.930 --> 00:12:29.817
maybe we keep replaying it in our head,

309
00:12:29.817 --> 00:12:31.457
"Oh, I could have gotten killed, I'm an idiot.

310
00:12:31.457 --> 00:12:33.090
"I shouldn't have done that."

311
00:12:33.090 --> 00:12:37.030
That is just kind of keeping that fear response going,

312
00:12:37.030 --> 00:12:38.564
chronically.
Wow.

313
00:12:38.564 --> 00:12:39.670
And that could happen for hours, days,

314
00:12:39.670 --> 00:12:41.140
weeks, years, you know,

315
00:12:41.140 --> 00:12:43.210
and this is where people, you know,

316
00:12:43.210 --> 00:12:45.830
it's not like we need a lifetime of psychotherapy

317
00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:47.050
for a fear response.

318
00:12:47.050 --> 00:12:49.420
Right, right.
But what we do need,

319
00:12:49.420 --> 00:12:51.710
is the ability to see the difference

320
00:12:51.710 --> 00:12:53.400
between a helpful fear response,

321
00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:56.640
and us literally getting spun out of control,

322
00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:58.740
because our mind's going out of control,

323
00:12:58.740 --> 00:13:01.220
making us continue to think about it.

324
00:13:01.220 --> 00:13:02.053
Right.

325
00:13:02.053 --> 00:13:04.380
And I think we're all experiencing, you know,

326
00:13:04.380 --> 00:13:07.240
some variation on those two things,

327
00:13:07.240 --> 00:13:09.900
over the past year,

328
00:13:09.900 --> 00:13:13.130
whether it's fear or unhealthy anxiety, you know,

329
00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:15.255
amidst a global pandemic,

330
00:13:15.255 --> 00:13:19.390
and our weapons of mass distraction,

331
00:13:19.390 --> 00:13:22.270
that are feeding us conflicting information,

332
00:13:22.270 --> 00:13:24.530
about X, Y and Z.

333
00:13:24.530 --> 00:13:27.380
I would suspect that this has created

334
00:13:27.380 --> 00:13:29.760
an unbelievably robust Petri dish,

335
00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:34.340
for you to really immerse yourself in the subject matter,

336
00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:35.880
in which you are an expert.

337
00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:39.980
And I know that early in the pandemic, like last May,

338
00:13:39.980 --> 00:13:43.190
you were writing pretty extensively about anxiety,

339
00:13:43.190 --> 00:13:45.030
and how we were grappling

340
00:13:45.030 --> 00:13:49.030
with how to manage this crazy shift

341
00:13:49.030 --> 00:13:50.280
in all of our lifestyles.

342
00:13:50.280 --> 00:13:52.693
But here we are almost a year later.

343
00:13:53.730 --> 00:13:58.730
It would seem to follow that there's less uncertainty now,

344
00:13:59.000 --> 00:14:00.740
perhaps the same amount of fear,

345
00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:04.700
but have you seen any kind of differentiation

346
00:14:04.700 --> 00:14:09.030
in how your patients or or the population at large,

347
00:14:09.030 --> 00:14:12.523
is kind of coping with COVID?

348
00:14:13.810 --> 00:14:15.310
So, I would say there are two main things

349
00:14:15.310 --> 00:14:19.320
that I'm noticing both in my clinic and then just at large.

350
00:14:19.320 --> 00:14:24.320
One is that there is that big spike of, "Wow, this is crazy.

351
00:14:24.847 --> 00:14:26.267
"This is really gonna be a pandemic.

352
00:14:26.267 --> 00:14:29.200
"Is it, wow, this really is a pandemic," you know?

353
00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:31.210
And then "How dangerous is this?"

354
00:14:31.210 --> 00:14:32.900
You know, "How infectious is this?"

355
00:14:32.900 --> 00:14:36.610
All of that, that uncertainty has gone down a little bit.

356
00:14:36.610 --> 00:14:39.090
And the death rate has gone down,

357
00:14:39.090 --> 00:14:40.360
when people figured out things

358
00:14:40.360 --> 00:14:44.330
like using steroids to help severely ill patients.

359
00:14:44.330 --> 00:14:46.000
So, that part has helped,

360
00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:48.080
yet we've seen the continual spikes,

361
00:14:48.080 --> 00:14:49.580
and these are intermittent ones,

362
00:14:49.580 --> 00:14:51.510
we don't know when they're gonna happen,

363
00:14:51.510 --> 00:14:53.170
with other forms of uncertainty,

364
00:14:53.170 --> 00:14:55.639
like the variants, "Oh," of "this variant popped up."

365
00:14:55.639 --> 00:14:59.200
And then those things feed a whole nother level

366
00:14:59.200 --> 00:15:00.940
of uncertainty.

367
00:15:00.940 --> 00:15:02.560
We've also seen things

368
00:15:02.560 --> 00:15:04.740
that really haven't changed that much

369
00:15:04.740 --> 00:15:06.470
in terms of the uncertainty.

370
00:15:06.470 --> 00:15:08.790
You know, small businesses, for example,

371
00:15:08.790 --> 00:15:10.520
the economy, for example,

372
00:15:10.520 --> 00:15:12.160
this is totally unprecedented.

373
00:15:12.160 --> 00:15:14.740
So, everybody is kind of feeling their way through this,

374
00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:18.100
whether it's a poor, small business owner,

375
00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.370
I've seen so many who've just like put their life savings,

376
00:15:20.370 --> 00:15:22.530
and they're like "Just one more month, just one more month."

377
00:15:22.530 --> 00:15:23.363
Right.

378
00:15:23.363 --> 00:15:24.690
And then they crash and burn.

379
00:15:24.690 --> 00:15:26.030
Or the feds,

380
00:15:26.030 --> 00:15:29.090
who are trying to figure out how to prop up the economy,

381
00:15:29.090 --> 00:15:32.330
without throwing us into whatever wild inflation

382
00:15:32.330 --> 00:15:33.163
or whatever.

383
00:15:33.163 --> 00:15:35.100
That's not my lane, so I don't know. (laughs)

384
00:15:35.100 --> 00:15:37.510
But the other piece that I've seen on top of this,

385
00:15:37.510 --> 00:15:39.740
is how people are coping.

386
00:15:39.740 --> 00:15:41.770
And I say, "Coping" with air quotes,

387
00:15:41.770 --> 00:15:45.930
because you've probably heard of the quarantine 15,

388
00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.050
and you know, where people have gained weight,

389
00:15:48.050 --> 00:15:52.480
and people are turning to these short-term coping strategies

390
00:15:52.480 --> 00:15:56.120
because are immediate and feel good in the moment.

391
00:15:56.120 --> 00:15:58.710
Whether it's drinking, you know, drinking has gone up,

392
00:15:58.710 --> 00:16:00.950
actually it's interesting, drinking has gone up

393
00:16:00.950 --> 00:16:02.940
in a significant part of the population,

394
00:16:02.940 --> 00:16:05.398
and some people have just basically cut out their drinking,

395
00:16:05.398 --> 00:16:08.770
probably due to lack of social resources,

396
00:16:08.770 --> 00:16:10.620
and the the usual places that they do.

397
00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:11.601
So, drinking's gone up,

398
00:16:11.601 --> 00:16:13.647
Netflix has had a quite a run.

399
00:16:13.647 --> 00:16:14.480
Yeah.

400
00:16:14.480 --> 00:16:16.580
You know, social media, all these things have gone up

401
00:16:16.580 --> 00:16:18.080
as coping mechanisms,

402
00:16:18.080 --> 00:16:20.530
that are probably gonna get laid down

403
00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:23.330
even harder as negative habits

404
00:16:23.330 --> 00:16:24.670
that people that are gonna have,

405
00:16:24.670 --> 00:16:27.200
that's gonna give this pandemic a long tail.

406
00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.480
And then, you know, I think the anxiety piece

407
00:16:29.480 --> 00:16:31.310
is gonna have an even longer tail.

408
00:16:31.310 --> 00:16:35.800
Some are describing this as the coming epidemic of anxiety.

409
00:16:35.800 --> 00:16:36.863
Right, right.

410
00:16:37.900 --> 00:16:39.920
What's interesting about what you just said,

411
00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:44.020
is this nexus between anxiety

412
00:16:44.020 --> 00:16:46.700
and addictive behavior patterns, right?

413
00:16:46.700 --> 00:16:49.380
I mean, it's logical if you really think about it,

414
00:16:49.380 --> 00:16:50.440
but this is something

415
00:16:50.440 --> 00:16:54.460
that your new book really gets into in depth,

416
00:16:54.460 --> 00:16:57.560
the extent to which addictive behaviors,

417
00:16:57.560 --> 00:17:01.950
really are, you know, a close cousin to chronic anxiety,

418
00:17:01.950 --> 00:17:04.310
and how that works neurochemically.

419
00:17:04.310 --> 00:17:07.890
Can you talk a little bit about what that connection is?

420
00:17:07.890 --> 00:17:08.770
Yeah, I'd be happy to.

421
00:17:08.770 --> 00:17:11.470
This was actually a big aha moment that I had

422
00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:14.550
several years ago now.

423
00:17:14.550 --> 00:17:15.950
So, just to set the stage

424
00:17:15.950 --> 00:17:18.670
as a budding addiction psychiatrist,

425
00:17:18.670 --> 00:17:21.070
I had a lot of patients, not only with addictions,

426
00:17:21.070 --> 00:17:22.250
but a lot of folks with anxiety.

427
00:17:22.250 --> 00:17:23.970
Anxiety and depression are the bread and butter

428
00:17:23.970 --> 00:17:25.800
of the psychiatric profession.

429
00:17:25.800 --> 00:17:26.633
Right.

430
00:17:26.633 --> 00:17:29.180
And I learned in medical school,

431
00:17:29.180 --> 00:17:32.870
to basically give people medications for anxiety.

432
00:17:32.870 --> 00:17:35.390
And the best medications out there,

433
00:17:35.390 --> 00:17:39.210
are actually in a class of antidepressants, ironically,

434
00:17:39.210 --> 00:17:41.900
because there aren't, you know, the anxiolytics,

435
00:17:41.900 --> 00:17:42.930
the benzodiazepines,

436
00:17:42.930 --> 00:17:44.460
are now no longer recommended

437
00:17:44.460 --> 00:17:47.030
as a first-line treatment for anxiety.

438
00:17:47.030 --> 00:17:49.510
So, the best evidence for these,

439
00:17:49.510 --> 00:17:53.130
shows that you need to treat about five people,

440
00:17:53.130 --> 00:17:54.640
before one person benefits.

441
00:17:54.640 --> 00:17:56.360
This is called the number needed to treat.

442
00:17:56.360 --> 00:17:58.530
And so as a psychiatrist,

443
00:17:58.530 --> 00:18:00.220
you can imagine it's not very satisfying

444
00:18:00.220 --> 00:18:01.277
for me to treat five people,

445
00:18:01.277 --> 00:18:03.700
and one person shows a significant benefit.

446
00:18:03.700 --> 00:18:06.890
So, I was struggling in my outpatient clinic,

447
00:18:06.890 --> 00:18:09.070
helping my patients with anxiety.

448
00:18:09.070 --> 00:18:10.560
And, you know, I was even trained

449
00:18:10.560 --> 00:18:12.260
in cognitive behavioral therapy.

450
00:18:12.260 --> 00:18:14.210
The response rate to cognitive behavioral therapy

451
00:18:14.210 --> 00:18:17.250
is about 50%, if you can get a good therapist, you know?

452
00:18:17.250 --> 00:18:18.332
So, there are a lot of barriers

453
00:18:18.332 --> 00:18:21.260
to good treatment for anxiety,

454
00:18:21.260 --> 00:18:23.710
so I was really struggling.

455
00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:26.960
And we were studying one of these apps

456
00:18:26.960 --> 00:18:28.440
that we developed for eating,

457
00:18:28.440 --> 00:18:29.550
it's called Eat Right Now.

458
00:18:29.550 --> 00:18:30.607
And somebody said, you know,

459
00:18:30.607 --> 00:18:34.487
"My habit pattern around eating goes like this.

460
00:18:34.487 --> 00:18:37.360
"I'm anxious and I eat," (laughs)

461
00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:39.660
you know, "Repeat."
(Rich laughs)

462
00:18:39.660 --> 00:18:40.560
And she said, you know,

463
00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:43.370
could you develop a program for anxiety?

464
00:18:43.370 --> 00:18:46.037
And I was thinking, "Well, you know, I'm a psychiatrist,

465
00:18:46.037 --> 00:18:47.900
"I generally prescribe medications."

466
00:18:47.900 --> 00:18:50.040
But as a researcher, you know,

467
00:18:50.040 --> 00:18:51.560
I knew a little something about habits.

468
00:18:51.560 --> 00:18:53.400
We'd been studying this for a long time.

469
00:18:53.400 --> 00:18:55.700
So, I went back and looked at the literature,

470
00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:57.390
and back in the '80s, right?

471
00:18:57.390 --> 00:18:58.700
This is when the Stones,

472
00:18:58.700 --> 00:19:00.800
this is how popular benzodiazepines were.

473
00:19:00.800 --> 00:19:04.020
Do you remember the song, "Mother Little's Helper"?

474
00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:04.853
Of course.

475
00:19:04.853 --> 00:19:05.686
Yes.

476
00:19:05.686 --> 00:19:07.500
Do you wanna sing a few bars for us? (laughs)

477
00:19:07.500 --> 00:19:10.800
No, I'm not going to subject anybody to my singing voice,

478
00:19:10.800 --> 00:19:12.180
but go ahead, feel free, Jud.
Yes.

479
00:19:12.180 --> 00:19:13.380
Well, I won't sing,

480
00:19:13.380 --> 00:19:15.587
but it goes something like,

481
00:19:15.587 --> 00:19:17.277
"She goes running to the shelter

482
00:19:17.277 --> 00:19:18.957
"of mother's little helper,

483
00:19:18.957 --> 00:19:20.817
"and it helps her through her day,

484
00:19:20.817 --> 00:19:22.160
"do, do, do, do, do, do."

485
00:19:22.160 --> 00:19:25.080
Yeah, so the Stones were singing about benzos,

486
00:19:25.080 --> 00:19:29.150
because they were so wildly popular in the '70s and '80s,

487
00:19:29.150 --> 00:19:32.810
and the psychiatric field was looking for new medications,

488
00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:35.680
so they came up with the SSRIs, you know?

489
00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:38.780
Prozac was, I think it was invented in '85

490
00:19:38.780 --> 00:19:40.210
or something like that.

491
00:19:40.210 --> 00:19:44.170
So, everybody's heralding the miracle of Prozac,

492
00:19:44.170 --> 00:19:47.280
while the Stones are singing about the benzos.

493
00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:48.850
And there was this guy at Penn State

494
00:19:48.850 --> 00:19:50.920
who was quietly studying anxiety,

495
00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:52.673
his name is Thomas Borkovec.

496
00:19:53.640 --> 00:19:56.520
And he said, "You know, I,"

497
00:19:56.520 --> 00:19:59.640
and he was very interested in the psychological aspects.

498
00:19:59.640 --> 00:20:01.047
And he said, "You know, I think anxiety

499
00:20:01.047 --> 00:20:03.577
"could actually be perpetuated,

500
00:20:03.577 --> 00:20:05.767
"in the same way as any other habit,

501
00:20:05.767 --> 00:20:07.680
"through negative reinforcement."

502
00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:09.410
And that's when all the bells went off for me,

503
00:20:09.410 --> 00:20:10.243
when I read that.

504
00:20:10.243 --> 00:20:11.076
You know, I went back,

505
00:20:11.076 --> 00:20:13.870
'cause I never learned this in residency or medical school,

506
00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:16.500
when I saw this literature, it was pretty solid.

507
00:20:16.500 --> 00:20:18.387
And I was thinking, "Wow, I never thought about that.

508
00:20:18.387 --> 00:20:21.830
"Could anxiety actually be driven habitually?"

509
00:20:21.830 --> 00:20:23.510
And so I started looking at that,

510
00:20:23.510 --> 00:20:24.710
and lo and behold,

511
00:20:24.710 --> 00:20:27.300
it can be negatively reinforced,

512
00:20:27.300 --> 00:20:29.950
just like any other bad habit.

513
00:20:29.950 --> 00:20:31.950
And that's where things really took off for me,

514
00:20:31.950 --> 00:20:34.350
and I started exploring that both in my research,

515
00:20:34.350 --> 00:20:35.894
and also in my clinic.

516
00:20:35.894 --> 00:20:38.880
Right, in the way that an addict

517
00:20:38.880 --> 00:20:42.900
experiences some level of emotional discomfort,

518
00:20:42.900 --> 00:20:47.560
and reaches for the substance to self-medicate that anxiety,

519
00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:51.850
is extremely similar in that you're feeling agitated,

520
00:20:51.850 --> 00:20:55.970
and you then try to ameliorate that,

521
00:20:55.970 --> 00:20:58.820
through any number of different behaviors, right?

522
00:20:58.820 --> 00:21:00.280
And this is something you talk about in the book.

523
00:21:00.280 --> 00:21:02.560
It's less about the anxiety,

524
00:21:02.560 --> 00:21:04.490
and it's more about the behavior

525
00:21:04.490 --> 00:21:05.830
that you're reaching out to,

526
00:21:05.830 --> 00:21:08.380
to self-medicate.

527
00:21:08.380 --> 00:21:09.213
Yeah, absolutely.

528
00:21:09.213 --> 00:21:10.380
So I've had a number of patients,

529
00:21:10.380 --> 00:21:12.380
and I think I write about one of them in the book,

530
00:21:12.380 --> 00:21:14.950
who were referred to me for alcohol use disorder.

531
00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:17.620
And in fact, their primary disorder,

532
00:21:17.620 --> 00:21:19.020
quote, unquote, I don't like that word,

533
00:21:19.020 --> 00:21:21.850
their primary issue, let's say, is anxiety.

534
00:21:21.850 --> 00:21:24.640
And so anxiety prompts them to drink,

535
00:21:24.640 --> 00:21:26.300
and then that gives them this brief relief,

536
00:21:26.300 --> 00:21:28.014
and then rinse and repeat every day.

537
00:21:28.014 --> 00:21:28.847
Right.

538
00:21:28.847 --> 00:21:29.927
But the other thing,

539
00:21:29.927 --> 00:21:32.830
and this comes back to the definition of anxiety,

540
00:21:32.830 --> 00:21:34.330
what Borkovec talked about,

541
00:21:34.330 --> 00:21:37.890
was that anxiety as a noun,

542
00:21:37.890 --> 00:21:39.250
so that negative feeling,

543
00:21:39.250 --> 00:21:41.920
that unpleasant feeling or emotion,

544
00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:45.940
can trigger worry as a mental behavior.

545
00:21:45.940 --> 00:21:46.970
And I wanna highlight that,

546
00:21:46.970 --> 00:21:48.840
because a lot of people think of behaviors

547
00:21:48.840 --> 00:21:51.340
as, you know, eating, smoking, drinking, whatever.

548
00:21:52.440 --> 00:21:55.240
Worry is a mental behavior,

549
00:21:55.240 --> 00:21:56.500
'cause we're doing something,

550
00:21:56.500 --> 00:21:58.890
it's just not obvious to everybody else.

551
00:21:58.890 --> 00:22:01.940
And that worry gives people a couple of things.

552
00:22:01.940 --> 00:22:03.910
One is a feeling of control,

553
00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:06.610
because at least they're doing something,

554
00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:10.300
and/or it can also distract them from that worst feeling,

555
00:22:10.300 --> 00:22:13.000
feeling of fear or anxiety.

556
00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.210
And so worry, as that mental behavior,

557
00:22:15.210 --> 00:22:19.090
can actually feed back and drive anxiety habit loops,

558
00:22:19.090 --> 00:22:20.650
that then, you know,

559
00:22:20.650 --> 00:22:23.010
when people realize they're not really in control,

560
00:22:23.010 --> 00:22:24.570
then they go over that event horizon

561
00:22:24.570 --> 00:22:25.910
into the black hole of anxiety,

562
00:22:25.910 --> 00:22:27.100
'cause they get worried,

563
00:22:27.100 --> 00:22:28.940
and then they get more anxious and they get more worried,

564
00:22:28.940 --> 00:22:31.830
and you know, and it just spirals out of control.

565
00:22:31.830 --> 00:22:32.870
Right.

566
00:22:32.870 --> 00:22:37.870
I grew up with a parent who is a chronic worrier,

567
00:22:38.250 --> 00:22:42.683
and, you know, in the way that a virus like COVID-19

568
00:22:45.810 --> 00:22:47.750
can get passed from person to person,

569
00:22:47.750 --> 00:22:52.010
these are emotions that travel virally,

570
00:22:52.010 --> 00:22:55.900
in a way that's perhaps worse than a virus,

571
00:22:55.900 --> 00:22:58.610
because of our social media apps, et cetera,

572
00:22:58.610 --> 00:22:59.920
and I know you've talked about this.

573
00:22:59.920 --> 00:23:04.450
But it took me a long time to deprogram,

574
00:23:04.450 --> 00:23:08.680
the amount of anxiety that was layered upon me

575
00:23:08.680 --> 00:23:10.000
throughout my youth,

576
00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:12.240
that made me a very afraid person.

577
00:23:12.240 --> 00:23:17.140
But I've come to recognize the extent to which this parent

578
00:23:17.140 --> 00:23:21.440
relied upon worry as basically, you know,

579
00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:26.440
a behavior that had some reward for her, my mother,

580
00:23:27.800 --> 00:23:29.750
in that it made it did make her feel

581
00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:30.910
like she was doing something,

582
00:23:30.910 --> 00:23:35.490
and it was all couched under this umbrella of love, right,

583
00:23:35.490 --> 00:23:37.980
as a way of rationalizing something,

584
00:23:37.980 --> 00:23:42.233
that was in truth, like, very unhealthy and pernicious.

585
00:23:43.390 --> 00:23:46.030
Yeah, well imagine a mother of a teenager.

586
00:23:46.030 --> 00:23:48.490
So, let's say Rich as a high school student

587
00:23:48.490 --> 00:23:51.460
goes out partying with his friends,

588
00:23:51.460 --> 00:23:53.960
and his mother worries all night,

589
00:23:53.960 --> 00:23:56.180
until she hears the door knob click

590
00:23:56.180 --> 00:23:58.150
or the garage door open, or whatever,

591
00:23:58.150 --> 00:23:59.640
and then she can get to sleep.

592
00:23:59.640 --> 00:24:01.210
Well, I'm gonna guess,

593
00:24:01.210 --> 00:24:05.100
that her worrying did not keep Rich safe.

594
00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:09.657
Mm mm, no, but it did color my worldview,

595
00:24:10.590 --> 00:24:15.590
and what I entertained as possible for my life.

596
00:24:15.670 --> 00:24:18.860
Like, it made me very conservative in my choices,

597
00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:22.630
like, my risk analysis was very conservative,

598
00:24:22.630 --> 00:24:24.550
and I think it limited me

599
00:24:24.550 --> 00:24:26.780
until I got enough adequate therapy,

600
00:24:26.780 --> 00:24:30.830
to kind of see more broadly what was actually going on.

601
00:24:30.830 --> 00:24:32.422
But I think what's interesting about that,

602
00:24:32.422 --> 00:24:34.200
it's almost, you know,

603
00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:35.790
I don't know if it's epigenetic,

604
00:24:35.790 --> 00:24:40.790
but the extent to which the anxiety or the worry or the fear

605
00:24:42.570 --> 00:24:45.880
that's carried by the people in your immediate environment,

606
00:24:45.880 --> 00:24:49.340
affect those around those people.

607
00:24:49.340 --> 00:24:50.173
Yeah, yeah.

608
00:24:50.173 --> 00:24:51.630
And it's so pervasive,

609
00:24:51.630 --> 00:24:53.300
that there's a scientific term for this,

610
00:24:53.300 --> 00:24:55.250
called social contagion.

611
00:24:55.250 --> 00:24:58.510
And I think like you alluded to with social media, you know,

612
00:24:58.510 --> 00:25:02.190
you can prevent the spread of a virus, you know,

613
00:25:02.190 --> 00:25:04.370
a physical virus by social distancing

614
00:25:04.370 --> 00:25:06.030
and masking and all those things,

615
00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:07.760
but somebody can sneeze on your brain

616
00:25:07.760 --> 00:25:09.460
from anywhere in the world,
Yeah.

617
00:25:09.460 --> 00:25:11.903
especially if we're going on social media,

618
00:25:11.903 --> 00:25:15.630
and constantly getting sneezed on.

619
00:25:15.630 --> 00:25:19.800
Yeah, I mean, 10 minutes before we started this podcast,

620
00:25:19.800 --> 00:25:22.040
I was looking at Twitter,

621
00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.060
which I shouldn't be doing but I was,

622
00:25:24.060 --> 00:25:28.317
and I watched the video of the the engine

623
00:25:29.720 --> 00:25:33.210
on the United Airlines flight from Denver to Honolulu,

624
00:25:33.210 --> 00:25:35.120
that like caught on fire and was exploding.

625
00:25:35.120 --> 00:25:37.000
And suddenly I was super, you know,

626
00:25:37.000 --> 00:25:39.410
I was like unnecessarily agitated.

627
00:25:39.410 --> 00:25:41.510
Like it's terrible to see that,

628
00:25:41.510 --> 00:25:42.960
and I'm glad that everybody's safe,

629
00:25:42.960 --> 00:25:45.550
and that that had a happy ending to it,

630
00:25:45.550 --> 00:25:49.064
but that caused me to enter into this conversation,

631
00:25:49.064 --> 00:25:51.000
in a state that, you know,

632
00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.020
I would have preferred to not be in,

633
00:25:53.020 --> 00:25:54.470
and I did it to myself.

634
00:25:54.470 --> 00:25:58.260
So, I think the lesson is we need to be more circumspect

635
00:25:58.260 --> 00:26:02.250
with these tools of social contagion.

636
00:26:02.250 --> 00:26:04.310
Yes, and I think here, you know,

637
00:26:04.310 --> 00:26:07.460
often people, they beat themselves up,

638
00:26:07.460 --> 00:26:08.910
and not saying this is the case for you,

639
00:26:08.910 --> 00:26:10.587
but I've seen a lot of people say,

640
00:26:10.587 --> 00:26:12.380
"Well, I shouldn't use social media so much,"

641
00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:13.797
or "I shouldn't go on social media

642
00:26:13.797 --> 00:26:16.880
"right before a big meeting, so I can calm down,"

643
00:26:16.880 --> 00:26:17.980
or whatever,

644
00:26:17.980 --> 00:26:19.810
and then they have another reason,

645
00:26:19.810 --> 00:26:21.197
they get in the habit loop of beating themselves up,

646
00:26:21.197 --> 00:26:23.070
Right.
because they can't

647
00:26:23.070 --> 00:26:24.570
just force themselves to stop,

648
00:26:24.570 --> 00:26:27.580
which goes back to this willpower myth,

649
00:26:27.580 --> 00:26:29.560
that's more myth than muscle.

650
00:26:29.560 --> 00:26:31.030
So, I think that's one important thing

651
00:26:31.030 --> 00:26:33.000
for folks to keep in mind.
Right.

652
00:26:33.000 --> 00:26:35.170
There may be other ways to help us get off social media,

653
00:26:35.170 --> 00:26:36.304
not just telling yourself
Right,

654
00:26:36.304 --> 00:26:37.610
that you shouldn't be on it.
Right, right, right.

655
00:26:37.610 --> 00:26:39.880
There is an interesting relationship

656
00:26:39.880 --> 00:26:42.980
between uncertainty and control.

657
00:26:42.980 --> 00:26:45.830
And I suspect that people have, you know,

658
00:26:45.830 --> 00:26:50.620
various relationships with their respective control issues,

659
00:26:50.620 --> 00:26:55.600
but those seem to be kind of intricately intertwined.

660
00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:56.950
When you enter into a situation,

661
00:26:56.950 --> 00:26:59.980
in which you feel like you don't have agency or control,

662
00:26:59.980 --> 00:27:02.860
that propagates a certain level of uncertainty,

663
00:27:02.860 --> 00:27:06.650
and somebody who has robust control issues,

664
00:27:06.650 --> 00:27:07.660
it seems to me,

665
00:27:07.660 --> 00:27:12.630
would be more susceptible to an anxious response.

666
00:27:12.630 --> 00:27:13.870
Does that track?

667
00:27:13.870 --> 00:27:15.170
Yeah, absolutely.
With the research?

668
00:27:15.170 --> 00:27:17.060
So, yeah, you can think of this,

669
00:27:17.060 --> 00:27:20.753
going back to this idea of, you know, our survival brains,

670
00:27:21.750 --> 00:27:25.110
the cave is a safe place because we're in control,

671
00:27:25.110 --> 00:27:28.100
you know, no saber tooth tigers in the cave.

672
00:27:28.100 --> 00:27:30.471
When we go out into the savanna,

673
00:27:30.471 --> 00:27:35.471
if we're just of the habitual mindset,

674
00:27:36.550 --> 00:27:38.390
of like, "I've gotta be in control,"

675
00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:42.180
we can actually, that uncertainty can feel like panic,

676
00:27:42.180 --> 00:27:45.190
and so we move from our safety zone into our panic zone.

677
00:27:45.190 --> 00:27:47.340
And then of course, wildly unthinking behavior,

678
00:27:47.340 --> 00:27:48.597
either we run back to the safety of the cave

679
00:27:48.597 --> 00:27:51.810
and we stave to death because we don't get food,

680
00:27:51.810 --> 00:27:53.680
or we freak out and run off a cliff,

681
00:27:53.680 --> 00:27:56.300
or do something that's not thinking.

682
00:27:56.300 --> 00:27:58.660
So here, I think it's really important,

683
00:27:58.660 --> 00:28:02.410
to see how we how we work with that uncertainty.

684
00:28:02.410 --> 00:28:07.170
And in fact, instead of moving into the panic zone,

685
00:28:07.170 --> 00:28:09.530
this is, it goes back to the lean in,

686
00:28:09.530 --> 00:28:11.990
if we can lean into what's happening,

687
00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:14.260
we can actually move into our growth zone.

688
00:28:14.260 --> 00:28:17.107
So, instead of thinking, "Oh no, this is happening again.

689
00:28:17.107 --> 00:28:18.740
"This is terrible, this is awful,"

690
00:28:18.740 --> 00:28:22.400
we can go, "Oh, this is different,"

691
00:28:22.400 --> 00:28:26.250
and we can start to see this as as that FGO,

692
00:28:26.250 --> 00:28:28.360
the freaking growth opportunity.

693
00:28:28.360 --> 00:28:29.337
Right.
You know?

694
00:28:29.337 --> 00:28:31.590
And I love Carol Dweck's work, you know?

695
00:28:31.590 --> 00:28:33.150
She was a professor
Right.

696
00:28:33.150 --> 00:28:33.983
at Stanford,

697
00:28:33.983 --> 00:28:35.704
talks about fixed versus growth mindset.

698
00:28:35.704 --> 00:28:38.700
You know, fixed mindset is that safety or the panic,

699
00:28:38.700 --> 00:28:41.900
where we're in some habitual way of being,

700
00:28:41.900 --> 00:28:45.670
either never venturing out or always freaking out.

701
00:28:45.670 --> 00:28:47.010
And that growth zone,

702
00:28:47.010 --> 00:28:50.520
is the, "Oh what can I learn from this?" zone.

703
00:28:50.520 --> 00:28:53.460
Right, but the first step in that process

704
00:28:53.460 --> 00:28:58.460
is it not having the ability to on some level,

705
00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:00.100
step outside yourself,

706
00:29:00.100 --> 00:29:02.460
so that you can recognize what's going on?

707
00:29:02.460 --> 00:29:04.260
I mean, you have this RAIN acronym,

708
00:29:04.260 --> 00:29:05.690
that kind of speaks to that.

709
00:29:05.690 --> 00:29:10.620
Like most of us are on some level of autopilot,

710
00:29:10.620 --> 00:29:13.970
where we're not recognizing the triggers,

711
00:29:13.970 --> 00:29:15.450
the behaviors, the results,

712
00:29:15.450 --> 00:29:17.490
we're just repeating a cycle,

713
00:29:17.490 --> 00:29:20.010
without any kind of mindfulness whatsoever.

714
00:29:20.010 --> 00:29:24.450
So, talk a little bit about how you initiate that process

715
00:29:24.450 --> 00:29:27.130
of becoming a little bit more self-aware

716
00:29:27.130 --> 00:29:29.390
of what is actually transpiring,

717
00:29:29.390 --> 00:29:33.680
so that you can then look at it as a growth opportunity,

718
00:29:33.680 --> 00:29:35.980
or recognize that there's something

719
00:29:35.980 --> 00:29:37.860
that you can actually do about it.

720
00:29:37.860 --> 00:29:41.160
Sure, I think of this as a three-step process.

721
00:29:41.160 --> 00:29:43.250
And this actually came to me

722
00:29:43.250 --> 00:29:45.600
as I was working more and more with my clinic patients

723
00:29:45.600 --> 00:29:48.970
and with our folks in our eating group, actually,

724
00:29:48.970 --> 00:29:52.010
before we'd even developed an anxiety program.

725
00:29:52.010 --> 00:29:53.470
And the first step,

726
00:29:53.470 --> 00:29:56.510
is really just kind of mapping out these habit loops.

727
00:29:56.510 --> 00:29:57.870
So, if we can't map them out,

728
00:29:57.870 --> 00:30:00.350
if we can't see that we're stuck,

729
00:30:00.350 --> 00:30:01.438
we have no way, you know,

730
00:30:01.438 --> 00:30:03.670
there's no way that we can use RAIN acronyms,

731
00:30:03.670 --> 00:30:04.937
or anything else to get out of them,

732
00:30:04.937 --> 00:30:06.440
because we don't know what we're running from.

733
00:30:06.440 --> 00:30:09.910
So here, maybe I'll give an example, a concrete example.

734
00:30:09.910 --> 00:30:13.390
I had a patient who was referred to me for anxiety,

735
00:30:13.390 --> 00:30:15.130
and when he walked into my office,

736
00:30:15.130 --> 00:30:16.340
I could see that he was anxious,

737
00:30:16.340 --> 00:30:18.170
so that was pretty straightforward.

738
00:30:18.170 --> 00:30:20.040
But when I started taking his history,

739
00:30:20.040 --> 00:30:21.640
he started describing how,

740
00:30:21.640 --> 00:30:23.480
when he would be driving on the highway,

741
00:30:23.480 --> 00:30:25.810
he felt like he was in a speeding bullet.

742
00:30:25.810 --> 00:30:29.330
And so he said, that thought was the trigger

743
00:30:29.330 --> 00:30:31.890
that triggered him to start avoiding driving on the highway,

744
00:30:31.890 --> 00:30:34.060
because he would get panic attacks.

745
00:30:34.060 --> 00:30:35.530
And so that avoidance behavior,

746
00:30:35.530 --> 00:30:37.580
helped him avoid getting panic attacks,

747
00:30:37.580 --> 00:30:39.940
and then he stopped driving on the highway.

748
00:30:39.940 --> 00:30:44.240
In fact, he had even gotten a bit anxious

749
00:30:44.240 --> 00:30:46.590
just driving the local roads to get to my office.

750
00:30:46.590 --> 00:30:47.660
So, the first thing we did,

751
00:30:47.660 --> 00:30:50.140
I pulled out a piece of paper and a pen,

752
00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:52.090
and I just wrote trigger behavior result

753
00:30:52.090 --> 00:30:52.923
out on a piece of paper.

754
00:30:52.923 --> 00:30:55.139
And I said, "Okay, let's go through this.

755
00:30:55.139 --> 00:30:56.464
"What's your trigger?"

756
00:30:56.464 --> 00:30:57.427
And he was like, "Those thoughts."

757
00:30:57.427 --> 00:30:58.260
"What's the behavior?"

758
00:30:58.260 --> 00:30:59.770
"Well, I avoid driving."

759
00:30:59.770 --> 00:31:02.067
And then what's the result, "Well, now I'm kind of stuck,

760
00:31:02.067 --> 00:31:04.493
"because I'm really limited in what I do,

761
00:31:04.493 --> 00:31:08.360
"yet it helped me avoid having future panic attacks."

762
00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:10.282
So, there's a great example

763
00:31:10.282 --> 00:31:13.860
of just taking five minutes, literally, to map these out.

764
00:31:13.860 --> 00:31:14.730
So, anybody can do this,

765
00:31:14.730 --> 00:31:16.643
they don't need a psychiatrist just to sit down with them

766
00:31:16.643 --> 00:31:17.500
to do it.
Right.

767
00:31:17.500 --> 00:31:18.700
But that's the first step.

768
00:31:18.700 --> 00:31:22.610
If we don't know our habit loops, we can't work with them.

769
00:31:22.610 --> 00:31:24.480
And so that's what I sent him home to do.

770
00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:27.380
I said, I gave him, we have an Unwinding Anxiety app,

771
00:31:27.380 --> 00:31:28.570
I sent him home with that,

772
00:31:28.570 --> 00:31:31.720
and I said, "Just start mapping out your habit loops."

773
00:31:31.720 --> 00:31:32.860
And interestingly,

774
00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:35.479
I'll just say a little bit more about him,

775
00:31:35.479 --> 00:31:38.070
he came back two weeks later.

776
00:31:38.070 --> 00:31:40.147
So I said, you know, "Let's set up a follow-up appointment

777
00:31:40.147 --> 00:31:40.980
"for two weeks."

778
00:31:40.980 --> 00:31:43.330
He came back two weeks later,

779
00:31:43.330 --> 00:31:44.850
and he said something really striking,

780
00:31:44.850 --> 00:31:47.117
that I had not expected him to say, okay?

781
00:31:47.117 --> 00:31:50.040
And this actually leads into that second step.

782
00:31:50.040 --> 00:31:53.060
So, first step is mapping out these habit loops.

783
00:31:53.060 --> 00:31:56.800
The second step is kind of seeing how rewarding they are.

784
00:31:56.800 --> 00:31:58.360
And what I failed to mention,

785
00:31:58.360 --> 00:32:01.570
was that this gentleman was 180 pounds overweight, okay,

786
