1140
00:45:41.090 --> 00:45:43.530
So here, what I would say as well,

1141
00:45:43.530 --> 00:45:45.480
what we do know in modern day,

1142
00:45:45.480 --> 00:45:47.919
is a little bit of neuroscience you know,

1143
00:45:47.919 --> 00:45:50.430
that wasn't known back in ancient Greece.

1144
00:45:50.430 --> 00:45:52.890
And one of the pieces that we do know,

1145
00:45:52.890 --> 00:45:54.250
is that reward-based learning

1146
00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:57.100
is the strongest mechanism in our brain.

1147
00:45:57.100 --> 00:45:58.620
So why not start there,

1148
00:45:58.620 --> 00:46:00.470
rather than the prefrontal cortex,

1149
00:46:00.470 --> 00:46:02.690
which ironically goes offline when we're stressed

1150
00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:04.390
or we're tired, or, you know?

1151
00:46:04.390 --> 00:46:06.019
That's where the HALT acronym comes from,

1152
00:46:06.019 --> 00:46:07.660
you know, hungry, angry, lonely, tired.

1153
00:46:07.660 --> 00:46:10.380
That's when our prefrontal cortex goes offline.

1154
00:46:10.380 --> 00:46:15.380
So, if that's not accessible or useful or strong enough,

1155
00:46:16.230 --> 00:46:18.300
why not tap into the reward-based learning

1156
00:46:18.300 --> 00:46:19.170
part of the brain?

1157
00:46:19.170 --> 00:46:21.660
And it too, is relatively simple,

1158
00:46:21.660 --> 00:46:24.730
and the research has been known for decades

1159
00:46:24.730 --> 00:46:26.320
about how this works.

1160
00:46:26.320 --> 00:46:28.210
So, basically there are these two researchers

1161
00:46:28.210 --> 00:46:30.850
in the '70s, Rescorla and Wagner.

1162
00:46:30.850 --> 00:46:33.330
They developed this model called the Rescorla-Wagner model

1163
00:46:33.330 --> 00:46:36.810
where we'd developed this, basically,

1164
00:46:36.810 --> 00:46:41.127
a prediction value or a reward value of different behaviors.

1165
00:46:41.127 --> 00:46:42.370
And the way this is set up,

1166
00:46:42.370 --> 00:46:44.410
is to help us not have to relearn everything

1167
00:46:44.410 --> 00:46:45.490
every day, you know?

1168
00:46:45.490 --> 00:46:47.833
So, if given a choice between two behaviors,

1169
00:46:47.833 --> 00:46:51.130
we don't have to try the A, and then try B,

1170
00:46:51.130 --> 00:46:52.910
and then compare the two, you know,

1171
00:46:52.910 --> 00:46:54.433
we've tried them enough before,

1172
00:46:54.433 --> 00:46:55.887
that we say, "Oh yeah, I'm gonna do B,

1173
00:46:55.887 --> 00:46:58.440
"because I know it's more rewarding than A."

1174
00:46:58.440 --> 00:47:00.750
So, our brains are gonna pick what's more rewarding.

1175
00:47:00.750 --> 00:47:02.990
So, for example, I don't know, tying our shoes,

1176
00:47:02.990 --> 00:47:05.770
let's say A is not tying your shoes, B is tying your shoes.

1177
00:47:05.770 --> 00:47:07.457
You realize if you tie your shoes, you don't trip,

1178
00:47:07.457 --> 00:47:10.870
it's more rewarding, we just do it as a habit, okay?

1179
00:47:10.870 --> 00:47:13.920
So, the only way to change a behavior,

1180
00:47:13.920 --> 00:47:16.930
is by updating that reward value,

1181
00:47:16.930 --> 00:47:21.260
and that reward value can get laid down for a long time.

1182
00:47:21.260 --> 00:47:22.430
I dunno, we could use eating

1183
00:47:22.430 --> 00:47:23.840
or smoking as an example, right?

1184
00:47:23.840 --> 00:47:26.640
So, think of all the times we've eaten cake, you know,

1185
00:47:26.640 --> 00:47:29.050
birthday parties, celebrations, all these things,

1186
00:47:29.050 --> 00:47:31.730
that gets laid down as this rewarding behavior,

1187
00:47:31.730 --> 00:47:33.110
and then we see cake and we eat cake,

1188
00:47:33.110 --> 00:47:35.330
'cause our brain says that's rewarding.

1189
00:47:35.330 --> 00:47:37.610
The only way to change that,

1190
00:47:37.610 --> 00:47:40.200
