and, you know, with a worse relationship

1298
00:51:39.670 --> 00:51:41.710
with my partner or whatever,

1299
00:51:41.710 --> 00:51:45.350
then that starts to take hold as a reduced reward value.

1300
00:51:45.350 --> 00:51:47.610
And if they can recall that,

1301
00:51:47.610 --> 00:51:48.687
then their brain says,

1302
00:51:48.687 --> 00:51:50.360
"Do I really wanna have a drink?"

1303
00:51:50.360 --> 00:51:53.500
And the same thing is true for the eating example.

1304
00:51:53.500 --> 00:51:56.037
It's like, "If I overeat again,

1305
00:51:56.037 --> 00:51:57.700
"what am I gonna get from this?"

1306
00:51:57.700 --> 00:52:00.150
So, I actually like the very simple,

1307
00:52:00.150 --> 00:52:03.130
I have folks in the step two of the book,

1308
00:52:03.130 --> 00:52:05.127
just ask the simple question,

1309
00:52:05.127 --> 00:52:06.710
"What do I get from this?"

1310
00:52:06.710 --> 00:52:07.543
Right.

1311
00:52:07.543 --> 00:52:09.949
And again, it's not intellectual it's experiential.

1312
00:52:09.949 --> 00:52:11.360
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

1313
00:52:11.360 --> 00:52:14.146
Like try to really connect with the emotional experience

1314
00:52:14.146 --> 00:52:17.523
of following through on that unhealthy behavior.

1315
00:52:17.523 --> 00:52:20.830
And one of the things that I've learned in recovery,

1316
00:52:20.830 --> 00:52:22.580
is called playing it through.

1317
00:52:22.580 --> 00:52:25.910
And it's not, it's about having a longer view

1318
00:52:25.910 --> 00:52:27.750
on like, "Okay, if I'm gonna drink,"

1319
00:52:27.750 --> 00:52:32.750
like, "Oh, I'm gonna ameliorate this emotional feeling

1320
00:52:34.167 --> 00:52:37.017
"of dis-ease that I'm experiencing right now.

1321
00:52:37.017 --> 00:52:38.990
"That's what's triggering me to do it."

1322
00:52:38.990 --> 00:52:41.037
But play it through like, "Okay, then tomorrow

1323
00:52:41.037 --> 00:52:42.877
"I'm gonna wake up and feel like shit,

1324
00:52:42.877 --> 00:52:44.717
"I'm not gonna be able to do my job,

1325
00:52:44.717 --> 00:52:48.057
"or study or whatever it is that I have in store.

1326
00:52:48.057 --> 00:52:51.410
"My partner is gonna give me a little bit of side-eye."

1327
00:52:51.410 --> 00:52:53.130
You know, like just like play it

1328
00:52:53.130 --> 00:52:54.980
all the way to its conclusion,

1329
00:52:54.980 --> 00:52:56.550
not just the reward,

1330
00:52:56.550 --> 00:53:00.153
but what's on the other side of that reward.

1331
00:53:00.153 --> 00:53:01.750
Absolutely, yes.

1332
00:53:01.750 --> 00:53:02.583
Yeah.

1333
00:53:04.030 --> 00:53:07.560
A big part of rewiring this reward system,

1334
00:53:07.560 --> 00:53:12.560
has to do with not just mindfulness,

1335
00:53:12.920 --> 00:53:15.590
but also curiosity and kindness.

1336
00:53:15.590 --> 00:53:16.830
So, I wanna spend a couple of minutes

1337
00:53:16.830 --> 00:53:18.223
talking about curiosity.

1338
00:53:19.100 --> 00:53:21.220
And what I loved about what you wrote in the book,

1339
00:53:21.220 --> 00:53:23.600
is there's a distinction,

1340
00:53:23.600 --> 00:53:25.390
there are two different kinds of curiosity.

1341
00:53:25.390 --> 00:53:27.070
There's deprivation curiosity,

1342
00:53:27.070 --> 00:53:28.510
and there's interest curiosity.

1343
00:53:28.510 --> 00:53:30.070
'Cause you hear a lot about curiosity

1344
00:53:30.070 --> 00:53:30.957
in the mindfulness space,

1345
00:53:30.957 --> 00:53:32.000
"Just be curious."

1346
00:53:32.000 --> 00:53:34.350
But it is a little bit more complicated

1347
00:53:34.350 --> 00:53:35.723
and nuanced than that.

1348
00:53:36.570 --> 00:53:38.270
It is, you know, and I'm laughing

1349
00:53:38.270 --> 00:53:41.050
because I didn't even know there were two types

1350
00:53:41.050 --> 00:53:43.970
until I think it was about two and a half years ago.

1351
00:53:43.970 --> 00:53:47.580
And I was just, you know, you're probably this way,

1352
00:53:47.580 --> 00:53:49.280
I was just a naturally curious kid.

1353
00:53:49.280 --> 00:53:50.790
Like, I loved to learn how things work.

1354
00:53:50.790 --> 00:53:53.040
I used to destroy my toys,

1355
00:53:53.040 --> 00:53:56.080
to see how they worked.
Uh huh.

1356
00:53:56.080 --> 00:53:57.987
And so I just thought, "Oh curiosity,

1357
00:53:57.987 --> 00:53:59.247
"yeah, there's one flavor to that."

1358
00:53:59.247 --> 00:54:02.020
But it turns out there's a fair amount of science

1359
00:54:02.020 --> 00:54:03.790
suggesting that there are two types,

1360
00:54:03.790 --> 00:54:06.210
and it's helpful to differentiate them,

1361
00:54:06.210 --> 00:54:08.490
because they both work in different ways.

1362
00:54:08.490 --> 00:54:12.200
So, the first flavor is deprivation curiosity,

1363
00:54:12.200 --> 00:54:15.068
which is basically a lack of information.

1364
00:54:15.068 --> 00:54:18.090
So, when we don't know the answer to something,

1365
00:54:18.090 --> 00:54:19.977
you know, this is our brain seeing,

1366
00:54:19.977 --> 00:54:22.060
"Oh, there's uncertainty here."

1367
00:54:22.060 --> 00:54:26.240
It is driven literally in a survival mechanism way,

1368
00:54:26.240 --> 00:54:27.940
same type of urges that we get,

1369
00:54:27.940 --> 00:54:31.010
when we go to get food or whatever,

1370
00:54:31.010 --> 00:54:33.570
we're driven to go get the answer to that.

1371
00:54:33.570 --> 00:54:36.140
You know, whether it's a trivia question or, you know,

1372
00:54:36.140 --> 00:54:37.570
where is water, you know,

1373
00:54:37.570 --> 00:54:39.626
'cause I don't know where water is.

1374
00:54:39.626 --> 00:54:42.590
And that can be negatively reinforced.

1375
00:54:42.590 --> 00:54:44.730
So, the trigger is, "I don't know the answer."

1376
00:54:44.730 --> 00:54:47.150
The behavior might be, you know, if it's a trivia question,

1377
00:54:47.150 --> 00:54:50.350
we'd go to our weapon of mass distraction and look it up.

1378
00:54:50.350 --> 00:54:53.750
And then the relief comes from knowing the answer.

1379
00:54:53.750 --> 00:54:57.360
So, I think of this as destination curiosity.

1380
00:54:57.360 --> 00:55:01.840
You know, once we get to the destination, we're satisfied,

1381
00:55:01.840 --> 00:55:03.170
but until we get there,

1382
00:55:03.170 --> 00:55:05.720
it's called deprivation because we feel deprived,

1383
00:55:05.720 --> 00:55:07.820
and that deprivation drives us,

1384
00:55:07.820 --> 00:55:10.210
to go and seek out that information

1385
00:55:10.210 --> 00:55:13.542
Right, like a heroin addict in a new city.

1386
00:55:13.542 --> 00:55:15.550
The deprivation curiosity

1387
00:55:15.550 --> 00:55:17.930
would be like, "Where am I gonna score?"

1388
00:55:17.930 --> 00:55:18.910
You know?
Yes.

1389
00:55:18.910 --> 00:55:21.900
And the level of intentionality and determination

1390
00:55:21.900 --> 00:55:25.771
that gets channeled into that curiosity,

1391
00:55:25.771 --> 00:55:29.043
could power a nuclear, could power a city, right?

1392
00:55:29.043 --> 00:55:31.270
Like that person is gonna figure it out

1393
00:55:31.270 --> 00:55:33.720
and find their heroin.

1394
00:55:33.720 --> 00:55:35.700
Not such a good curiosity.

1395
00:55:35.700 --> 00:55:37.210
Yes, exactly, yeah.

1396
00:55:37.210 --> 00:55:38.900
Don't get in their way, basically. (laughs)

1397
00:55:38.900 --> 00:55:41.490
Yeah. (laughs)

1398
00:55:41.490 --> 00:55:42.680
They will be like that bus,

1399
00:55:42.680 --> 00:55:44.330
and you'll be hit by that bus.

1400
00:55:44.330 --> 00:55:46.380
Yeah, 100%.

1401
00:55:46.380 --> 00:55:50.860
All right, so distinguish that from interest curiosity.

1402
00:55:50.860 --> 00:55:53.390
So, the other flavor is interest curiosity.

1403
00:55:53.390 --> 00:55:56.870
And in contrast to the destination,

1404
00:55:56.870 --> 00:55:58.770
I think of this as the journey,

1405
00:55:58.770 --> 00:56:03.770
where we are just finding that it's very joyful

1406
00:56:05.290 --> 00:56:08.210
to discover new things to learn, right?

1407
00:56:08.210 --> 00:56:12.740
So, it doesn't matter, there's no destination in mind,

1408
00:56:12.740 --> 00:56:15.430
we're just happy to learn new things.

1409
00:56:15.430 --> 00:56:17.450
It's kind of like being in our growth zone.

1410
00:56:17.450 --> 00:56:19.730
When we can go, instead of go, "Oh no,"

1411
00:56:19.730 --> 00:56:23.300
we can go, "Oh, what can I learn from this?"

1412
00:56:23.300 --> 00:56:25.690
This can be in any day situation,

1413
00:56:25.690 --> 00:56:27.660
where we're out of our comfort zone,

1414
00:56:27.660 --> 00:56:30.210
or it could be when we're specifically, you know,

1415
00:56:30.210 --> 00:56:31.420
learning a new topic,

1416
00:56:31.420 --> 00:56:34.820
or learning some new sport, even.

1417
00:56:34.820 --> 00:56:36.270
It's like, "Oh," you know?

1418
00:56:36.270 --> 00:56:38.680
Learning some new sport, and we're struggling,

1419
00:56:38.680 --> 00:56:39.777
we can go, "Oh, this sucks, this is terrible."

1420
00:56:39.777 --> 00:56:43.280
We can go, "Oh, well that was different, "

1421
00:56:43.280 --> 00:56:45.160
you know, "let me try this,"

1422
00:56:45.160 --> 00:56:46.930
and we can be open to that experience.

1423
00:56:46.930 --> 00:56:49.080
And it feels very, very different.

1424
00:56:49.080 --> 00:56:51.425
So, instead of it being negatively reinforced,

1425
00:56:51.425 --> 00:56:54.527
so the deprivation is driven by this urge, you know,

1426
00:56:54.527 --> 00:56:57.310
"Get out of my way, I gotta find this,"

1427
00:56:57.310 --> 00:57:00.260
the interest is very much more open,

1428
00:57:00.260 --> 00:57:03.614
where we are curious and like, "Oh," you know,

1429
00:57:03.614 --> 00:57:06.040
and that actually puts us in growth mindset,

1430
00:57:06.040 --> 00:57:08.020
so we can actually learn a lot more,

1431
00:57:08.020 --> 00:57:10.657
rather than just, "Okay, here's this one, you know,

1432
00:57:10.657 --> 00:57:11.917
"I need to find where the heroin is.

1433
00:57:11.917 --> 00:57:13.320
"Now, I found it, done."

1434
00:57:13.320 --> 00:57:14.580
We haven't really learned a whole lot

1435
00:57:14.580 --> 00:57:17.670
except that one specific thing.

1436
00:57:17.670 --> 00:57:18.503
Right.

1437
00:57:18.503 --> 00:57:22.840
I would think that that type of curiosity,

1438
00:57:22.840 --> 00:57:27.840
and the extent to which somebody is able to develop

1439
00:57:31.410 --> 00:57:34.980
the open-mindedness required to explore that,

1440
00:57:34.980 --> 00:57:37.390
is gonna calibrate pretty closely

1441
00:57:37.390 --> 00:57:42.390
with how much that challenges their core values,

1442
00:57:42.900 --> 00:57:44.310
or their identity.

1443
00:57:44.310 --> 00:57:46.730
I mean, this is something that I talked about

1444
00:57:46.730 --> 00:57:48.640
in a different context with Adam Grant,

1445
00:57:48.640 --> 00:57:51.800
just like how do you have conversations with people

1446
00:57:51.800 --> 00:57:53.900
that see the world differently?

1447
00:57:53.900 --> 00:57:57.740
Well, one of the things you do is you lead with curiosity.

1448
00:57:57.740 --> 00:57:59.790
If you're in a situation

1449
00:57:59.790 --> 00:58:01.810
in which that person's worldview

1450
00:58:01.810 --> 00:58:04.060
is so different from your own,

1451
00:58:04.060 --> 00:58:06.860
that it feels like a threat to who you are,

1452
00:58:06.860 --> 00:58:09.500
or how you see the world,

1453
00:58:09.500 --> 00:58:10.860
it's gonna be more difficult

1454
00:58:10.860 --> 00:58:13.030
to marshal that level of curiosity.

1455
00:58:13.030 --> 00:58:16.010
So, how does that map onto anxiety?

1456
00:58:16.010 --> 00:58:19.160
If you have a patient or an individual,

1457
00:58:19.160 --> 00:58:22.270
who is so self identifying with their anxiety

1458
00:58:22.270 --> 00:58:25.000
as part and parcel of who they are,

1459
00:58:25.000 --> 00:58:29.780
I would imagine that that's going to be a tougher case

1460
00:58:29.780 --> 00:58:33.850
than somebody who's relatively less aware

1461
00:58:33.850 --> 00:58:37.053
of the extent to which anxiety is driving their behavior.

1462
00:58:38.010 --> 00:58:41.100
Yes, so I think even going to that example

1463
00:58:41.100 --> 00:58:44.840
of trying to have a conversation with somebody, you know,

1464
00:58:44.840 --> 00:58:47.820
if we are trying to put ourselves in their shoes,

1465
00:58:47.820 --> 00:58:51.170
and truly understand where they're coming from,

1466
00:58:51.170 --> 00:58:52.520
as compared to how, you know,

1467
00:58:52.520 --> 00:58:54.330
we don't have a destination in mind,

1468
00:58:54.330 --> 00:58:57.030
we're not trying to beat them over the head

1469
00:58:57.030 --> 00:58:58.100
with our point of view,

1470
00:58:58.100 --> 00:59:01.820
and keep them yelling at them until they acquiesce,

1471
00:59:01.820 --> 00:59:03.339
or, you know,

1472
00:59:03.339 --> 00:59:05.900
that curiosity itself,

1473
00:59:05.900 --> 00:59:07.552
going back to social contagion,

1474
00:59:07.552 --> 00:59:10.230
can be contagious,

1475
00:59:10.230 --> 00:59:12.427
where they see, "Oh, this person

1476
00:59:12.427 --> 00:59:14.200
"really wants to understand who I am."

1477
00:59:14.200 --> 00:59:16.160
And of course, people love to talk about themselves,

1478
00:59:16.160 --> 00:59:19.260
and so you can actually capitalize on that piece,

1479
00:59:19.260 --> 00:59:21.410
where, you know, we're sneezing curiosity

1480
00:59:21.410 --> 00:59:23.510
on that other person.

1481
00:59:23.510 --> 00:59:27.830
And so that can be translated into the clinical setting,

1482
00:59:27.830 --> 00:59:31.270
where as a therapist or as a psychiatrist,

1483
00:59:31.270 --> 00:59:35.410
it's really helpful if I am truly curious about my patients,

1484
00:59:35.410 --> 00:59:38.120
as compared to them walking in the door, looking anxious,

1485
00:59:38.120 --> 00:59:39.787
and then I just peg them as, "Oh yeah, you're anxious.

1486
00:59:39.787 --> 00:59:40.830
"Take this medication."

1487
00:59:40.830 --> 00:59:41.663
Right.
Wow,

1488
00:59:41.663 --> 00:59:42.890
that's not gonna be that helpful.

1489
00:59:42.890 --> 00:59:44.690
So, if I'm truly curious,

1490
00:59:44.690 --> 00:59:46.250
and the nice thing about people

1491
00:59:46.250 --> 00:59:47.910
is everybody has their own story,

1492
00:59:47.910 --> 00:59:49.910
so there's tons to be curious about.

1493
00:59:49.910 --> 00:59:51.380
And I'm sure you're this way,

1494
00:59:51.380 --> 00:59:55.500
it's just like hearing people's story makes my day,

1495
00:59:55.500 --> 00:59:57.030
it just fills me with energy,

1496
00:59:57.030 --> 00:59:59.337
because I'm learning something about somebody, you know,

1497
00:59:59.337 --> 01:00:02.240
and everybody has a unique and interesting story.

1498
01:00:02.240 --> 01:00:05.157
So, it starts there as, you know, with the therapist,

1499
01:00:05.157 --> 01:00:08.320
or even having a conversation with somebody.

1500
01:00:08.320 --> 01:00:10.000
It's like, "Oh," you know?

1501
01:00:10.000 --> 01:00:11.950
So, when somebody walks in my door and they're anxious,

1502
01:00:11.950 --> 01:00:14.670
if I can sneeze curiosity on them,

1503
01:00:14.670 --> 01:00:19.270
it helps them open up to starting to see their own,

1504
01:00:19.270 --> 01:00:22.050
you know, their own habit loops around anxiety, for example.

1505
01:00:22.050 --> 01:00:22.883
Right.

1506
01:00:22.883 --> 01:00:24.130
And then the next thing I can do,

1507
01:00:24.130 --> 01:00:26.110
once they've kind of gotten a taste of that,

1508
01:00:26.110 --> 01:00:29.000
is I can give them specific things to practice,

1509
01:00:29.000 --> 01:00:30.043
to awaken that curiosity.

1510
01:00:30.043 --> 01:00:31.400
Because we all have it,

1511
01:00:31.400 --> 01:00:34.720
it's not like something you have to buy at the store.

1512
01:00:34.720 --> 01:00:36.167
You know, "I'm a little short,

1513
01:00:36.167 --> 01:00:37.561
"I have to go pick that up at the store."

1514
01:00:37.561 --> 01:00:41.110
This is about just awakening something that we already have,

1515
01:00:41.110 --> 01:00:43.240
that's our own natural capacity.

1516
01:00:43.240 --> 01:00:46.460
And so simple things like if somebody is anxious,

1517
01:00:46.460 --> 01:00:49.120
we even have a tool built into our Unwinding Anxiety,

1518
01:00:49.120 --> 01:00:50.810
the app, to do this.

1519
01:00:50.810 --> 01:00:53.960
We have somebody feel into their anxiety,

1520
01:00:53.960 --> 01:00:56.100
and then ask themselves, you know,

1521
01:00:56.100 --> 01:00:58.010
when they feel into where they feel it strongest

1522
01:00:58.010 --> 01:00:59.260
in their body,

1523
01:00:59.260 --> 01:01:02.547
ask themselves, "Is this stronger on the right side

1524
01:01:02.547 --> 01:01:04.347
"or the left side of my body?"

1525
01:01:05.520 --> 01:01:10.380
And the idea there is to go, "Hmm, well, I don't know."

1526
01:01:10.380 --> 01:01:12.600
It doesn't matter what the answer is,

1527
01:01:12.600 --> 01:01:16.020
but that, "Hmm" is that first hint of curiosity

1528
01:01:16.020 --> 01:01:18.770
that they're starting to awaken curiosity,

1529
01:01:18.770 --> 01:01:21.233
instead of being sucked into their anxiety.

1530
01:01:22.207 --> 01:01:23.700
And what this can help do,

1531
01:01:23.700 --> 01:01:25.990
is it's like the observer effect in physics,

1532
01:01:25.990 --> 01:01:27.625
you know when they were trying to measure

1533
01:01:27.625 --> 01:01:29.280
the mass of an electron,

1534
01:01:29.280 --> 01:01:31.490
they would hit it with photons, with light.

1535
01:01:31.490 --> 01:01:32.760
And by hitting it with photons,

1536
01:01:32.760 --> 01:01:34.930
you're actually changing the velocity

1537
01:01:34.930 --> 01:01:36.340
and therefore the measurement.

1538
01:01:36.340 --> 01:01:37.980
So, they call this the observer effect.

1539
01:01:37.980 --> 01:01:40.670
By observing, you're actually gonna affect the results.

1540
01:01:40.670 --> 01:01:44.380
And the same thing is true in psychology or psychiatry.

1541
01:01:44.380 --> 01:01:47.450
If we are very identified with our thoughts and emotions,

1542
01:01:47.450 --> 01:01:49.390
it's hard to change them,

1543
01:01:49.390 --> 01:01:50.940
but if we can observe them,

1544
01:01:50.940 --> 01:01:53.730
by observing, we are less identified.

1545
01:01:53.730 --> 01:01:56.977
And so we can start to see, "Oh there's anxiety.

1546
01:01:56.977 --> 01:01:59.817
"Oh, that's on more on the right side or the left side

1547
01:01:59.817 --> 01:02:00.650
"of my body."

1548
01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:02.760
So, we can see, start to see it more clearly,

1549
01:02:02.760 --> 01:02:05.550
simply through that awakening process,

1550
01:02:05.550 --> 01:02:07.560
and that's truly fostered by curiosity.

1551
01:02:07.560 --> 01:02:09.830
It helps us be able to step back and observe more.

1552
01:02:09.830 --> 01:02:11.340
Hmm, yeah.

1553
01:02:11.340 --> 01:02:14.220
And it seems that it would follow

1554
01:02:14.220 --> 01:02:19.220
that curiosity works as a salve to the shame response,

1555
01:02:22.180 --> 01:02:26.290
to experiencing these types of emotions, right?

1556
01:02:26.290 --> 01:02:29.220
I'm imagining the person who is in,

1557
01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:30.810
perhaps they're having a panic attack,

1558
01:02:30.810 --> 01:02:33.200
or some kind of anxious response.

1559
01:02:33.200 --> 01:02:35.090
They don't really wanna reflect on it

1560
01:02:35.090 --> 01:02:39.245
because then they start to feel bad about it and themselves.

1561
01:02:39.245 --> 01:02:43.410
So, I've seen people just, they shut down emotionally,

1562
01:02:43.410 --> 01:02:48.050
or they avoid any kind of conversational confrontation

1563
01:02:48.050 --> 01:02:49.080
about that,

1564
01:02:49.080 --> 01:02:54.080
because it provokes so much difficult emotion around it.

1565
01:02:55.810 --> 01:03:00.810
But curiosity seems to just kind of allow that to evaporate.

1566
01:03:01.360 --> 01:03:02.840
It creates space, right?

1567
01:03:02.840 --> 01:03:06.453
And so maybe that's where the kindness piece comes in?

1568
01:03:07.470 --> 01:03:09.650
Yes, I think of these two,

1569
01:03:09.650 --> 01:03:14.000
so curiosity and kindness as being complimentary buckets.

1570
01:03:14.000 --> 01:03:18.220
And so there are many pieces or things

1571
01:03:18.220 --> 01:03:19.380
in each of those buckets,

1572
01:03:19.380 --> 01:03:20.790
that can help support each of these.

1573
01:03:20.790 --> 01:03:23.230
So, for example, with kindness,

1574
01:03:23.230 --> 01:03:26.020
gratitude is a great way to reflect on kindness,

1575
01:03:26.020 --> 01:03:28.290
whether it was the kindness of others or whatever,

1576
01:03:28.290 --> 01:03:31.930
that helps us tap into that feeling of kindness.

1577
01:03:31.930 --> 01:03:33.390
So, on a broad scale,

1578
01:03:33.390 --> 01:03:35.920
my lab's actually done a study,

1579
01:03:35.920 --> 01:03:38.660
where we queried several hundred people,

1580
01:03:38.660 --> 01:03:41.900
to see where on the reward value spectrum,

1581
01:03:41.900 --> 01:03:45.160
different emotions are, or different mind states,

1582
01:03:45.160 --> 01:03:46.510
and we found uniformly,

1583
01:03:46.510 --> 01:03:48.250
probably not surprisingly,

1584
01:03:48.250 --> 01:03:51.460
that anxiety, frustration, anger,

1585
01:03:51.460 --> 01:03:56.440
all feel less rewarding and more closed down,

1586
01:03:56.440 --> 01:04:00.050
than I think kindness and curiosity, basically.

1587
01:04:00.050 --> 01:04:02.870
And so connected, you can think of connection,

1588
01:04:02.870 --> 01:04:06.190
as being, you know, in the bucket of kindness, let's say.

1589
01:04:06.190 --> 01:04:08.980
So here, when we look at this,

1590
01:04:08.980 --> 01:04:11.440
uniformly, kindness and curiosity

1591
01:04:11.440 --> 01:04:14.070
feel more open and expanded

1592
01:04:14.070 --> 01:04:19.030
than feeling anxious or disconnected or whatever,

1593
01:04:19.030 --> 01:04:20.330
or being, you know, being mean,

1594
01:04:20.330 --> 01:04:23.200
or being the object of somebody's anger.

1595
01:04:23.200 --> 01:04:26.900
So here, just being able to tap into what it feels like

1596
01:04:27.770 --> 01:04:31.210
to have kindness bestowed upon us,

1597
01:04:31.210 --> 01:04:32.957
or to be kind to others,

1598
01:04:32.957 --> 01:04:35.240
and truly being kind in a selfless way,

1599
01:04:35.240 --> 01:04:36.577
not thinking, you know, "I'm gonna hold the door,

1600
01:04:36.577 --> 01:04:40.970
"so she's likely to smile at me," or whatever,

1601
01:04:40.970 --> 01:04:43.480
you know, not looking for anything in return.

1602
01:04:43.480 --> 01:04:46.730
So, that's where these two share this overlap of openness,

1603
01:04:46.730 --> 01:04:49.750
of connection, of expansion,

1604
01:04:49.750 --> 01:04:51.740
that is just more rewarding,

1605
01:04:51.740 --> 01:04:54.400
because it feels better than being closed down

1606
01:04:54.400 --> 01:04:56.370
or disconnected.

1607
01:04:56.370 --> 01:05:00.930
Is there a difference between kindness towards others,

1608
01:05:00.930 --> 01:05:04.210
versus self-kindness,

1609
01:05:04.210 --> 01:05:08.420
in terms of how that affects someone's anxiety?

1610
01:05:08.420 --> 01:05:11.120
I would say pragmatically,

1611
01:05:11.120 --> 01:05:13.570
it can be more challenging for a lot of people

1612
01:05:13.570 --> 01:05:16.810
to tap into the feeling of self-kindness,

1613
01:05:16.810 --> 01:05:19.770
but the feeling itself, whether it's kindness toward others

1614
01:05:19.770 --> 01:05:20.840
or kindness toward ourselves,

1615
01:05:20.840 --> 01:05:22.900
that expanding feeling is the same.

1616
01:05:22.900 --> 01:05:24.089
Right, right.

1617
01:05:24.089 --> 01:05:29.089
It seems that a gesture of kindness to another person,

1618
01:05:29.510 --> 01:05:33.019
irregardless of motive, still has an impact.

1619
01:05:33.019 --> 01:05:35.900
And it could be just a simple gesture,

1620
01:05:35.900 --> 01:05:40.790
but to do that to yourself is very unfamiliar

1621
01:05:40.790 --> 01:05:41.930
for a lot of people,

1622
01:05:41.930 --> 01:05:44.790
they feel like it's indulgent, or they don't deserve it.

1623
01:05:44.790 --> 01:05:49.790
It seems like it's a more complicated web to unravel.

1624
01:05:50.270 --> 01:05:51.680
Yeah, I think it can be.

1625
01:05:51.680 --> 01:05:52.810
And I see this a lot,

1626
01:05:52.810 --> 01:05:54.960
when people are working with their eating habits,

1627
01:05:54.960 --> 01:05:59.960
where they mix up self-indulgence with self-kindness,

1628
01:06:00.180 --> 01:06:01.013
you know?

1629
01:06:01.013 --> 01:06:03.830
And so they have a bad day and their habit is to stress eat,

1630
01:06:03.830 --> 01:06:06.140
or eat some ice cream at the end of the day.

1631
01:06:06.140 --> 01:06:07.937
And someone, you know, a lot of people say,

1632
01:06:07.937 --> 01:06:11.417
"Well, how do I know whether it's, it feels like

1633
01:06:11.417 --> 01:06:12.667
"I'm just being kind to myself

1634
01:06:12.667 --> 01:06:15.020
"by eating that tub of ice cream."

1635
01:06:15.020 --> 01:06:17.897
And I say, "Check in with yourself,

1636
01:06:17.897 --> 01:06:19.700
"how kind it is that?"

1637
01:06:19.700 --> 01:06:21.110
You know, "What do you get from that?"

1638
01:06:21.110 --> 01:06:23.490
And, you know, typically it's they numb themselves

1639
01:06:23.490 --> 01:06:24.323
for a little bit,

1640
01:06:24.323 --> 01:06:25.600
but then they feel guilty,

1641
01:06:25.600 --> 01:06:26.810
and they don't feel, you know,

1642
01:06:26.810 --> 01:06:28.960
they get the gut bomb and all of these things.

1643
01:06:28.960 --> 01:06:31.300
And when they add up the reward value,

1644
01:06:31.300 --> 01:06:33.697
they can clearly see, "Oh, that's self-indulgence,

1645
01:06:33.697 --> 01:06:35.100
"that's a habit,"

1646
01:06:35.100 --> 01:06:37.870
versus, you know, "I've had a hard day."

1647
01:06:37.870 --> 01:06:40.570
And then they can ask themselves, "Well, what do I need?"

1648
01:06:40.570 --> 01:06:41.403
You know?

1649
01:06:41.403 --> 01:06:42.860
Maybe a little bit of ice cream

1650
01:06:42.860 --> 01:06:44.130
but not a whole tub, you know?

1651
01:06:44.130 --> 01:06:45.350
Maybe a little bit of chocolate.

1652
01:06:45.350 --> 01:06:47.570
And when they really pay attention,

1653
01:06:47.570 --> 01:06:49.810
they can actually find that pleasure plateau,

1654
01:06:49.810 --> 01:06:51.760
where, you know, it's just enough,

1655
01:06:51.760 --> 01:06:52.890
and then they can stop,

1656
01:06:52.890 --> 01:06:54.820
simply by bringing awareness to that.

1657
01:06:54.820 --> 01:06:58.130
And so I think that's one of the big challenges for people,

1658
01:06:58.130 --> 01:07:00.590
is to differentiate that self-indulgence

1659
01:07:00.590 --> 01:07:02.050
versus the self kindness.

1660
01:07:02.050 --> 01:07:04.100
Right, and I think built into something

1661
01:07:04.100 --> 01:07:05.080
that you just said,

1662
01:07:05.080 --> 01:07:06.340
which is super important,

1663
01:07:06.340 --> 01:07:09.480
is that no matter what behavior,

1664
01:07:09.480 --> 01:07:11.380
errant behavior you're indulging in,

1665
01:07:11.380 --> 01:07:13.300
you are getting something out of that.

1666
01:07:13.300 --> 01:07:15.470
You are being rewarded, right?

1667
01:07:15.470 --> 01:07:16.970
Like, it's not all bad.

1668
01:07:16.970 --> 01:07:19.850
There's a reason why this is locked in for you.

1669
01:07:19.850 --> 01:07:24.460
And so the process of reconfiguring that whole thing,

1670
01:07:24.460 --> 01:07:26.210
is identifying that reward.

1671
01:07:26.210 --> 01:07:29.440
I mean, it goes back to your paradigm here,

1672
01:07:29.440 --> 01:07:32.787
but understanding like, "Oh, I eat ice cream,

1673
01:07:32.787 --> 01:07:35.100
"because, you know, I hate myself."

1674
01:07:35.100 --> 01:07:39.250
Well, no, actually it is doing something positive for you

1675
01:07:39.250 --> 01:07:40.730
in a certain way,

1676
01:07:40.730 --> 01:07:42.780
that also leads to negative effects.

1677
01:07:42.780 --> 01:07:47.130
But recognizing what that positive thing is, I think,

1678
01:07:47.130 --> 01:07:49.570
is an important step.

1679
01:07:49.570 --> 01:07:50.560
Absolutely.

1680
01:07:50.560 --> 01:07:53.780
And that actually leads us to the third step,

1681
01:07:53.780 --> 01:07:57.010
which is, you know, for a lot of us,

1682
01:07:57.010 --> 01:07:59.030
that's been the plateau of our pleasure,

1683
01:07:59.030 --> 01:08:01.000
of our, you know, of a reward,

1684
01:08:01.000 --> 01:08:03.960
where we think excitement or eating ice cream,

1685
01:08:03.960 --> 01:08:06.360
or binge watching Netflix or whatever,

1686
01:08:06.360 --> 01:08:08.160
is the peak of experience, you know?

1687
01:08:09.290 --> 01:08:10.810
But there is such a thing as too much

1688
01:08:10.810 --> 01:08:12.090
of a good thing, right?

1689
01:08:12.090 --> 01:08:13.700
We can't go on vacation forever.

1690
01:08:13.700 --> 01:08:15.110
We can't have sex forever.

1691
01:08:15.110 --> 01:08:17.885
We can't just keep eating ice cream forever, you know?

1692
01:08:17.885 --> 01:08:21.290
Our body says "Enough!"
Uh huh.

1693
01:08:21.290 --> 01:08:25.340
So here, we can learn to see not only how unrewarding

1694
01:08:25.340 --> 01:08:28.320
some of, you know, when we peak at that pleasure plateau,

1695
01:08:28.320 --> 01:08:30.200
and then start going off the other edge,

1696
01:08:30.200 --> 01:08:32.330
we can see how unrewarding these things are,

1697
01:08:32.330 --> 01:08:35.590
including habits around guilt and all these things,

1698
01:08:35.590 --> 01:08:37.390
which are echo habit loops, typically,

1699
01:08:37.390 --> 01:08:39.790
on top of these other habit loops.

1700
01:08:39.790 --> 01:08:42.580
And then we can go back to that brain paradigm

1701
01:08:42.580 --> 01:08:45.950
and give ourself something better.

1702
01:08:45.950 --> 01:08:49.600
So, I think of this as the BBO, the bigger better offer.

1703
01:08:49.600 --> 01:08:52.550
So, our brain, again, setting up reward hierarchies,

1704
01:08:52.550 --> 01:08:55.720
if it starts to see that one behavior is not as rewarding,

1705
01:08:55.720 --> 01:08:57.570
there's that negative prediction error,

1706
01:08:57.570 --> 01:09:00.760
it's gonna say, "Okay, give me something better."

1707
01:09:00.760 --> 01:09:04.300
And this is where kindness and curiosity come in.

1708
01:09:04.300 --> 01:09:06.580
So, we can, when we're kind to ourselves,

1709
01:09:06.580 --> 01:09:08.360
versus beating ourselves up,

1710
01:09:08.360 --> 01:09:10.760
which feels better, you know?
Right.

1711
01:09:10.760 --> 01:09:14.740
When we have a fixed view versus being curious

1712
01:09:14.740 --> 01:09:16.030
in a conversation,

1713
01:09:16.030 --> 01:09:17.350
which feels better?

1714
01:09:17.350 --> 01:09:19.140
Which one helps us feel more connected

1715
01:09:19.140 --> 01:09:19.973
with the other person?

1716
01:09:19.973 --> 01:09:24.070
Which helps us learn something or discover something new

1717
01:09:24.070 --> 01:09:25.270
about that person?

1718
01:09:25.270 --> 01:09:27.720
To our brains, literally, it's a no brainer.

1719
01:09:27.720 --> 01:09:30.530
You mentioned bringing mindfulness to all of this,

1720
01:09:30.530 --> 01:09:35.530
which is kind of like the umbrella component to all of this.

1721
01:09:36.190 --> 01:09:37.140
I think for a lot of people,

1722
01:09:37.140 --> 01:09:39.470
we talked about this extensively last time you were on,

1723
01:09:39.470 --> 01:09:42.010
everybody should go back and listen to that episode.

1724
01:09:42.010 --> 01:09:43.635
But for a lot of people,

1725
01:09:43.635 --> 01:09:46.780
that lands kind of in an esoteric space,

1726
01:09:46.780 --> 01:09:47.760
like what does that mean?

1727
01:09:47.760 --> 01:09:51.580
Like, "Oh, bring mindfulness to my bad behavior pattern."

1728
01:09:51.580 --> 01:09:53.560
Like, walk us through

1729
01:09:53.560 --> 01:09:55.650
what specifically you're talking about,

1730
01:09:55.650 --> 01:09:59.010
and how that can be helpful.

1731
01:09:59.010 --> 01:10:01.520
You know, less and less these days,

1732
01:10:01.520 --> 01:10:03.580
I use the term mindfulness,

1733
01:10:03.580 --> 01:10:05.960
because it's a concept, right?

1734
01:10:05.960 --> 01:10:08.050
It was actually from this old term, Sati,

1735
01:10:08.050 --> 01:10:09.190
which means to remember,

1736
01:10:09.190 --> 01:10:10.950
and you're thinking how does "to remember"

1737
01:10:10.950 --> 01:10:13.010
have to do with, you know,

1738
01:10:13.010 --> 01:10:14.410
paying attention and all this stuff.

1739
01:10:14.410 --> 01:10:15.410
So, in modern day,

1740
01:10:15.410 --> 01:10:18.770
there are many definitions of mindfulness, you know,

1741
01:10:18.770 --> 01:10:20.860
Jon Kabat-Zinn, most famous, I think,

1742
01:10:20.860 --> 01:10:22.380
in the Western sphere,

1743
01:10:22.380 --> 01:10:23.570
for giving this definition,

1744
01:10:23.570 --> 01:10:25.870
of paying attention on purpose, in the present moment,

1745
01:10:25.870 --> 01:10:27.620
non-judgmentally.

1746
01:10:27.620 --> 01:10:29.820
Well, honestly, that's a pretty long definition

1747
01:10:29.820 --> 01:10:31.480
that could trip people up along the way.

1748
01:10:31.480 --> 01:10:33.200
What does it mean to be nonjudgmental?

1749
01:10:33.200 --> 01:10:35.480
So here, I like to break it down

1750
01:10:35.480 --> 01:10:37.260
into its element components,

1751
01:10:37.260 --> 01:10:38.720
and there are two,

1752
01:10:38.720 --> 01:10:40.720
you can think of this as two sides of a coin.

1753
01:10:40.720 --> 01:10:41.840
One is awareness.

1754
01:10:41.840 --> 01:10:43.370
We all know what awareness is, right,

1755
01:10:43.370 --> 01:10:45.240
we're either aware or we're not aware.

1756
01:10:45.240 --> 01:10:47.967
And the other you've already touched on, which is curiosity.

1757
01:10:47.967 --> 01:10:52.160
And so you can think of the positive framing of non-judgment

1758
01:10:52.160 --> 01:10:54.490
as curiosity, being curious,

1759
01:10:54.490 --> 01:10:56.380
not jumping to conclusions,

1760
01:10:56.380 --> 01:10:58.540
not assuming we know what's happening,

1761
01:10:58.540 --> 01:11:01.940
truly bringing a fresh perspective.

1762
01:11:01.940 --> 01:11:04.390
Which is why in a lot of the Buddhist circles, you know,

1763
01:11:04.390 --> 01:11:06.680
they talk about beginner's mind in Zen,

1764
01:11:06.680 --> 01:11:08.450
or they talk, in vipassana, they talk,

1765
01:11:08.450 --> 01:11:11.260
vipassana literally means seeing clearly,

1766
01:11:11.260 --> 01:11:13.740
where we've taken off our subjective bias glasses,

1767
01:11:13.740 --> 01:11:15.760
where we see the world a certain way,

1768
01:11:15.760 --> 01:11:16.593
we've taken those off,

1769
01:11:16.593 --> 01:11:19.780
and we're just seeing things without any of those biases.

1770
01:11:19.780 --> 01:11:24.243
I want to pivot and talk a little bit about teens.

1771
01:11:27.520 --> 01:11:31.868
There is an epidemic of teen anxiety going on right now.

1772
01:11:31.868 --> 01:11:33.730
I've got a 17 year old daughter,

1773
01:11:33.730 --> 01:11:36.435
who's having an unbelievably difficult time

1774
01:11:36.435 --> 01:11:38.730
with the pandemic,

1775
01:11:38.730 --> 01:11:42.480
and having to do all of her schooling on Zoom.

1776
01:11:42.480 --> 01:11:45.460
We tell our kids, you know, "Get off the screens,"

1777
01:11:45.460 --> 01:11:48.713
now it's "Get on the screens," it's very confusing.

1778
01:11:49.930 --> 01:11:51.310
My daughter's an extrovert,

1779
01:11:51.310 --> 01:11:52.940
she's unable to see her friends

1780
01:11:52.940 --> 01:11:55.270
to the extent that she would like,

1781
01:11:55.270 --> 01:11:57.110
she doesn't have the freedoms,

1782
01:11:57.110 --> 01:11:58.670
that perhaps she would have,

1783
01:11:58.670 --> 01:12:00.880
if things were a little bit different,

1784
01:12:00.880 --> 01:12:03.740
and it's taking quite a toll,

1785
01:12:03.740 --> 01:12:06.210
and we've been struggling with trying to figure out

1786
01:12:06.210 --> 01:12:09.340
how to, you know be of service to her

1787
01:12:09.340 --> 01:12:10.400
in the most productive way.

1788
01:12:10.400 --> 01:12:15.400
So, I'm interested in whether you have any experience

1789
01:12:15.680 --> 01:12:17.040
working with teens specifically,

1790
01:12:17.040 --> 01:12:18.960
or what it is about the teen experience

1791
01:12:18.960 --> 01:12:21.860
that perhaps is a little bit qualitatively different

1792
01:12:21.860 --> 01:12:24.603
from maybe, you know, people like you and I.

1793
01:12:25.560 --> 01:12:28.960
So, we've done a little bit of research with teens,

1794
01:12:28.960 --> 01:12:30.910
for example, teenage smokers,

1795
01:12:30.910 --> 01:12:32.680
and we've done a little bit of pilot work

1796
01:12:32.680 --> 01:12:34.280
with our Unwinding Anxiety program

1797
01:12:34.280 --> 01:12:37.690
to make sure it was accessible to teenagers,

1798
01:12:37.690 --> 01:12:40.520
but by no means am I an adolescent psychiatrist,

1799
01:12:40.520 --> 01:12:42.730
so I just want to start with that.

1800
01:12:42.730 --> 01:12:46.740
What I can say from my own experience as an anxious teen,

1801
01:12:46.740 --> 01:12:48.960
you know, we moved around a bit when I was a kid,

1802
01:12:48.960 --> 01:12:51.370
and that's can be challenging, (laughs)

1803
01:12:51.370 --> 01:12:52.800
you know, in terms of being anxious,

1804
01:12:52.800 --> 01:12:54.330
going into a new school, you know,

1805
01:12:54.330 --> 01:12:56.740
not knowing anybody and all that stuff.

1806
01:12:56.740 --> 01:13:01.280
What I can say is that it's really, really challenging.

1807
