2314
01:33:53.783 --> 01:33:58.200
that I talked with Andrew Huberman, neuroscientist,

2315
01:33:58.200 --> 01:34:03.100
was the way in which you can use eye movement

2316
01:34:03.100 --> 01:34:07.899
to kind of essentially like, reset your state.

2317
01:34:07.899 --> 01:34:10.940
Do you have thoughts on how all of that works?

2318
01:34:10.940 --> 01:34:13.000
Have you practiced that or studied that at all?

2319
01:34:13.000 --> 01:34:15.160
I've not practiced or studied it,

2320
01:34:15.160 --> 01:34:16.810
but I've been at conferences.

2321
01:34:16.810 --> 01:34:19.640
So, the most common technique that I've seen

2322
01:34:19.640 --> 01:34:22.560
that's been studied, is EMDR,

2323
01:34:22.560 --> 01:34:23.393
what is it?

2324
01:34:23.393 --> 01:34:26.170
Eye movement, desensitization, response or something.

2325
01:34:26.170 --> 01:34:29.400
Right, like the lateral, is that the lateral eye movement?

2326
01:34:29.400 --> 01:34:30.507
Yeah, yeah.
Yeah, mm hmm.

2327
01:34:30.507 --> 01:34:32.930
And the idea there that I understand,

2328
01:34:32.930 --> 01:34:34.920
and so I could be getting this wrong,

2329
01:34:34.920 --> 01:34:37.370
is that one core aspect of that,

2330
01:34:37.370 --> 01:34:42.330
is helping people anchor on physical experience.

2331
01:34:42.330 --> 01:34:44.910
So, when you have somebody move their eyes laterally,

2332
01:34:44.910 --> 01:34:47.210
from this side, to this side, to this side,

2333
01:34:47.210 --> 01:34:52.210
they have to focus on doing a task that is physical,

2334
01:34:52.760 --> 01:34:55.460
and it gets them out of their thinking head, right,

2335
01:34:55.460 --> 01:34:57.950
into their physical body.

2336
01:34:57.950 --> 01:34:59.809
And if you think of this,

2337
01:34:59.809 --> 01:35:03.860
by focusing on something like that,

2338
01:35:03.860 --> 01:35:06.600
you can take up all of the working memory

2339
01:35:06.600 --> 01:35:08.670
in your dorsolateral prefrontal cortex,

2340
01:35:08.670 --> 01:35:09.710
that part's not important,

2341
01:35:09.710 --> 01:35:12.170
but like in this working memory part of our brain,

2342
01:35:12.170 --> 01:35:13.610
and kind of reboot the system,

2343
01:35:13.610 --> 01:35:15.350
because our brains only have a certain amount

2344
01:35:15.350 --> 01:35:16.183
of working memory,

2345
01:35:16.183 --> 01:35:18.500
a certain amount of RAM so to speak.

2346
01:35:18.500 --> 01:35:20.640
So we can use things like EMDR,

2347
01:35:20.640 --> 01:35:22.700
or other eye movement techniques,

2348
01:35:22.700 --> 01:35:24.960
but you can even think of this more broadly.

2349
01:35:24.960 --> 01:35:26.690
One practice related to the breath

2350
01:35:26.690 --> 01:35:28.320
that I love, in particular,

2351
01:35:28.320 --> 01:35:30.230
is called the five finger breathing.

2352
01:35:30.230 --> 01:35:31.210
I love this in particular,

2353
01:35:31.210 --> 01:35:33.040
because people can teach it to their young kids,

2354
01:35:33.040 --> 01:35:34.570
and then have their kids walk them through it

2355
01:35:34.570 --> 01:35:36.120
when they're anxious.

2356
01:35:36.120 --> 01:35:38.380
And the idea is to just add, you know,

2357
01:35:38.380 --> 01:35:40.510
take the index finger of one hand,

2358
01:35:40.510 --> 01:35:43.350
and place it at the base of the pinky on the other hand.

2359
01:35:43.350 --> 01:35:47.680
And then as they breathe in, feel the physical sensations

2360
01:35:47.680 --> 01:35:49.950
of their breath and their fingers,

2361
01:35:49.950 --> 01:35:52.840
and look at their fingers at the same time,

2362
01:35:52.840 --> 01:35:54.790
pause at the top, as they breathe out,

2363
01:35:54.790 --> 01:35:56.950
trace down the inside of their pinky,

2364
01:35:56.950 --> 01:35:57.800
as they breathe in,

2365
01:35:57.800 --> 01:36:00.210
trace up the outside of their ring finger,

2366
01:36:00.210 --> 01:36:01.210
you know, and down.

2367
01:36:01.210 --> 01:36:03.300
So, for example, if you make five full breaths,

2368
01:36:03.300 --> 01:36:04.410
you've traced your whole hand.

2369
01:36:04.410 --> 01:36:06.310
If you do 10, you can trace back from your thumb

2370
01:36:06.310 --> 01:36:07.360
to your pinky.

2371
01:36:07.360 --> 01:36:11.240
And what that does is it takes up a lot of working memory,

2372
01:36:11.240 --> 01:36:12.490
'cause we're paying attention,

2373
01:36:12.490 --> 01:36:14.731
we're seeing, we're feeling three different aspects

2374
01:36:14.731 --> 01:36:15.950
of our experience, you know,

2375
01:36:15.950 --> 01:36:17.600
two fingers and our breath,

2376
01:36:17.600 --> 01:36:20.500
and that reboots the whole working memory system.

2377
01:36:20.500 --> 01:36:23.540
And at the same time, paying attention to breathing,

2378
01:36:23.540 --> 01:36:27.010
often helps regulate our physiology where it calms down.

2379
01:36:27.010 --> 01:36:29.990
So, if those worry thoughts come back in,

2380
01:36:29.990 --> 01:36:34.370
they actually need a level of arousal that meets them,

2381
01:36:34.370 --> 01:36:35.203
you know?

2382
01:36:35.203 --> 01:36:37.550
We're used to being worried and feeling worried, you know,

2383
01:36:37.550 --> 01:36:39.510
thinking worried, feeling worried.

2384
01:36:39.510 --> 01:36:40.980
Well, if we're thinking worried

2385
01:36:40.980 --> 01:36:43.070
and we're not feeling worried,

2386
01:36:43.070 --> 01:36:46.690
that body is gonna say, "Hey, that's just a thought,"

2387
01:36:46.690 --> 01:36:49.150
and we can identify and let go of thoughts more easily

2388
01:36:49.150 --> 01:36:52.440
because there's a mismatch between the arousal.

2389
01:36:52.440 --> 01:36:54.380
So, and it's the physical body

2390
01:36:54.380 --> 01:36:56.590
that drives experience and behavior,

2391
01:36:56.590 --> 01:36:58.100
more than thinking brain.

2392
01:36:58.100 --> 01:36:59.810
So, that one's gonna win,

2393
01:36:59.810 --> 01:37:02.640
if we can really help regulate that physiology.

2394
01:37:02.640 --> 01:37:03.530
Sure.

2395
01:37:03.530 --> 01:37:06.672
It's so crazy that these, I mean, it's so simple

2396
01:37:06.672 --> 01:37:09.317
that it's like, "Okay, breathe,

2397
01:37:09.317 --> 01:37:10.730
"and like, look at your fingers,"

2398
01:37:10.730 --> 01:37:14.970
and you can have like such a powerful impact

2399
01:37:14.970 --> 01:37:16.713
on the mind.

2400
01:37:17.770 --> 01:37:19.130
There's so much-
Yeah, it's called science,

2401
01:37:19.130 --> 01:37:19.963
you know?
Right.

2402
01:37:19.963 --> 01:37:23.083
If we actually understand how it works. (laughs)

2403
01:37:24.030 --> 01:37:27.310
Final thing, I mean, there's so much interesting science

2404
01:37:27.310 --> 01:37:28.810
that's occurring right now,

2405
01:37:28.810 --> 01:37:31.500
in the study of the mind and the brain,

2406
01:37:31.500 --> 01:37:34.780
and yet so much of it remains unmapped.

2407
01:37:34.780 --> 01:37:36.940
There's so much that we still are grappling with

2408
01:37:36.940 --> 01:37:38.420
trying to understand.

2409
01:37:38.420 --> 01:37:40.660
From your perspective, like, what is the study,

2410
01:37:40.660 --> 01:37:42.560
that you would like to see performed,

2411
01:37:42.560 --> 01:37:44.360
that has yet been performed,

2412
01:37:44.360 --> 01:37:46.050
that would help elucidate, you know,

2413
01:37:46.050 --> 01:37:49.663
a lot of the things that you are curious about?

2414
01:37:50.636 --> 01:37:54.030
Well, there are a gazillion things that I'm curious about,

2415
01:37:54.030 --> 01:37:57.040
yet one thing that really interested in,

2416
01:37:57.040 --> 01:38:01.770
is this idea of personalized medicine, right?

2417
01:38:01.770 --> 01:38:05.260
This has been a buzz word in medicine for a long time

2418
01:38:05.260 --> 01:38:09.180
and it hasn't really yet come to fruition,

2419
01:38:09.180 --> 01:38:12.300
where, you know, the idea would be to take somebody's genes,

2420
01:38:12.300 --> 01:38:13.133
and all these, you know,

2421
01:38:13.133 --> 01:38:14.720
their environment and behavior,

2422
01:38:14.720 --> 01:38:16.860
and find what medication works best for them,

2423
01:38:16.860 --> 01:38:19.140
or tailor a medication to them.

2424
01:38:19.140 --> 01:38:22.850
If you approach that same personalized medicine,

2425
01:38:22.850 --> 01:38:25.300
away from the taking a pill perspective,

2426
01:38:25.300 --> 01:38:27.143
which is where I focus more,

2427
01:38:28.040 --> 01:38:31.010
the idea here is can we actually personalize medicine

2428
01:38:31.010 --> 01:38:33.648
through understanding someone's habitual reactions

2429
01:38:33.648 --> 01:38:35.160
to their world?

2430
01:38:35.160 --> 01:38:37.330
And we did one pilot study

2431
01:38:37.330 --> 01:38:39.850
that gave us some really interesting data,

2432
01:38:39.850 --> 01:38:42.430
that says to me that this is possible,

2433
01:38:42.430 --> 01:38:44.450
where we can actually take a,

2434
01:38:44.450 --> 01:38:46.250
what we're calling a psychological phenotype,

2435
01:38:46.250 --> 01:38:49.009
of asking people just 19 questions,

2436
01:38:49.009 --> 01:38:51.920
at baseline before they start a program,

2437
01:38:51.920 --> 01:38:54.280
and we can predict who's gonna do better,

2438
01:38:54.280 --> 01:38:55.650
than somebody else,

2439
01:38:55.650 --> 01:38:57.810
and with a pretty striking result.

2440
01:38:57.810 --> 01:38:59.870
And so what my next experiment,

2441
01:38:59.870 --> 01:39:01.420
that I wanna do,

2442
01:39:01.420 --> 01:39:03.590
is to do this at scale,

2443
01:39:03.590 --> 01:39:06.830
where we really look at, you know,

2444
